WEBVTT

1
00:00:36,578 --> 00:00:37,495
[static crackles]

2
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:51,968
[woman 1] Walk faster, people.

3
00:00:52,052 --> 00:00:54,679
[man 1] You want to be in?
Go, all of you. Come on.

4
00:00:54,763 --> 00:00:57,223
-[girl 1] Can Spice come?
-[man 1] Sure. Go ahead.

5
00:00:57,307 --> 00:00:58,808
-Spice.
-[boy] Spicy!

6
00:00:58,892 --> 00:01:00,810
-[girl 2] Hi.
-[children laugh]

7
00:01:00,894 --> 00:01:03,438
-[woman 1] The more the merrier.
-[man 1] It's <i>Wizard of Oz </i>time.

8
00:01:03,521 --> 00:01:05,857
-[children and adults speak indistinctly]
-[man 2] Okay!

9
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:07,025
[man 3 coughs]

10
00:01:07,108 --> 00:01:09,444
[man 2 whistles] You guys ready?

11
00:01:10,653 --> 00:01:12,113
-[child] Yeah!
-Come on!

12
00:01:12,197 --> 00:01:13,406
[man 3] Here they come.

13
00:01:24,459 --> 00:01:25,710
[man 1] Let him go.

14
00:01:26,252 --> 00:01:27,504
[horse whinnies]

15
00:01:27,587 --> 00:01:29,798
-Oh, okay.
-[girl] He's wagging.

16
00:01:29,881 --> 00:01:32,550
-[woman 2] Aaron.
-[man 1] Aaron, back up a bit.

17
00:01:33,301 --> 00:01:34,385
Okay.

18
00:01:34,469 --> 00:01:36,513
[children laugh]

19
00:01:36,596 --> 00:01:37,931
[child 1] Come on.

20
00:01:38,431 --> 00:01:39,516
[child 2] I'll get it.

21
00:01:40,433 --> 00:01:43,144
[man 1] That's good.
Aaron, go in and get it.

22
00:01:43,228 --> 00:01:45,396
Get down in there and get that boat.

23
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:47,774
[child laughs] Look at him.

24
00:01:47,857 --> 00:01:49,234
[intriguing music plays]

25
00:01:49,317 --> 00:01:52,028
[Aaron] Ugh. What if there was
toxic waste in here?

26
00:01:52,112 --> 00:01:53,279
[man] Don't even joke.

27
00:01:53,363 --> 00:01:55,198
[child 3] He'd die. [chuckles]

28
00:01:56,658 --> 00:01:58,660
[intriguing music intensifies]

29
00:03:34,422 --> 00:03:35,798
[children play in distance]

30
00:03:45,725 --> 00:03:47,727
[door creaks open]

31
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:07,914
[man] Somebody has to keep track
of where everything is buried.

32
00:04:09,791 --> 00:04:14,963
It's not just poison in the earth,
it's poison of the spirit as well.

33
00:04:27,809 --> 00:04:29,811
-[button clicks]
-[VCR whirs]

34
00:04:32,063 --> 00:04:33,273
[boy on video] <i>On three?</i>

35
00:04:33,356 --> 00:04:34,732
[boy inhales sharply]

36
00:04:35,483 --> 00:04:37,694
<i>One of the reasons</i>
<i>the Wallkill River is a sewer</i>

37
00:04:37,777 --> 00:04:41,864
<i>is because of the Orange County Landfill,</i>
<i>situated on the riverbank.</i>

38
00:04:43,533 --> 00:04:44,534
<i>Shit!</i>

39
00:04:45,285 --> 00:04:47,287
-[girl] <i>Oops.</i>
-<i>Shit!</i>

40
00:04:47,370 --> 00:04:51,541
[man] To me, it's more than just
a record of environmental crime.

41
00:04:52,417 --> 00:04:55,586
All the work that we were doing
on the documentaries back then,

42
00:04:56,671 --> 00:05:00,800
it's this archetype of young people
rising up to the challenge,

43
00:05:00,883 --> 00:05:04,470
investigating what the problems
are in their world

44
00:05:04,554 --> 00:05:06,597
and doing something about it.

45
00:05:08,182 --> 00:05:10,768
[girl] <i>Good evening,</i>
<i>and welcome to MHS TV</i>

46
00:05:10,852 --> 00:05:13,438
<i>from Middletown High School.</i>
<i>My name is Yvonne Kolinski,</i>

47
00:05:13,521 --> 00:05:16,107
<i>and tonight we have</i>
<i>a visual history of Middletown,</i>

48
00:05:16,190 --> 00:05:18,693
<i>a science experiment,</i>
<i>and some other surprises.</i>

49
00:05:18,776 --> 00:05:20,778
["Seether" by Veruca Salt plays]

50
00:05:27,327 --> 00:05:29,495
[director] Have you ever heard
of Middletown, New York?

51
00:05:29,579 --> 00:05:31,164
Um, maybe.

52
00:05:31,247 --> 00:05:34,000
I never heard of it,
probably because I work too much.

53
00:05:34,083 --> 00:05:35,126
[both] Never heard of it.

54
00:05:35,710 --> 00:05:36,961
No, I'm sorry.

55
00:05:37,045 --> 00:05:39,672
-[both laugh]
-They don't know where it is.

56
00:05:41,549 --> 00:05:44,802
Okay, so not everyone
has heard of Middletown, New York.

57
00:05:44,886 --> 00:05:46,179
I don't get it.

58
00:05:46,262 --> 00:05:48,765
Everybody up there
has heard of New York City.

59
00:05:49,349 --> 00:05:52,143
Straight up the river, 60 miles.
Come on, I'll show you.

60
00:05:52,226 --> 00:05:54,228
<i>♪ …keep her on a short leash ♪</i>

61
00:05:54,312 --> 00:05:55,313
<i>♪ I try… ♪</i>

62
00:05:55,396 --> 00:05:58,232
One hour and 30 minutes. Not bad.

63
00:05:58,316 --> 00:06:04,489
<i>♪ I try to ram her into the ground, yeah ♪</i>

64
00:06:08,576 --> 00:06:11,037
<i>♪ Can't fight the seether ♪</i>

65
00:06:11,954 --> 00:06:14,624
<i>♪ Can't fight the seether ♪</i>

66
00:06:15,458 --> 00:06:18,169
<i>♪ Can't fight the seether, I can't… ♪</i>

67
00:06:18,252 --> 00:06:20,880
[man 1] We moved to Middletown
when I was three.

68
00:06:20,963 --> 00:06:23,007
It was blue-collar.

69
00:06:23,091 --> 00:06:25,051
{\an8}You know, every house looked
exactly the same.

70
00:06:28,054 --> 00:06:31,641
{\an8}We were living in New York City,
and there was a story

71
00:06:31,724 --> 00:06:34,811
{\an8}that my brother got,
like, jacked at the park

72
00:06:34,894 --> 00:06:36,687
{\an8}for his Big Wheel or his bicycle,

73
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:40,400
and my parents decided
we're going to move to the suburbs.

74
00:06:40,483 --> 00:06:42,527
[people cheer and applaud]

75
00:06:42,610 --> 00:06:45,780
[man 2] Back then, Middletown
was primarily known for dairy farming.

76
00:06:45,863 --> 00:06:48,741
It was a bucolic setting, I guess.

77
00:06:48,825 --> 00:06:51,035
There was a canopy
over most of the streets in town,

78
00:06:51,119 --> 00:06:53,746
{\an8}so you'd always feel like you were
driving through tunnels of trees.

79
00:06:53,830 --> 00:06:57,834
[Rachel] We had our fair share
of sketchy spots, like anywhere else.

80
00:06:57,917 --> 00:07:01,170
An episode of <i>Cops</i>
was shot at the Orange Plaza Mall.

81
00:07:01,254 --> 00:07:04,924
I think it was, like, the first time
we saw Middletown on TV.

82
00:07:05,007 --> 00:07:08,177
<i>♪ …till I'm foaming at the mouth ♪</i>

83
00:07:08,261 --> 00:07:10,972
<i>♪ Can't find the seether… ♪</i>

84
00:07:12,056 --> 00:07:14,934
[man 3] You know, growing up there,
it doesn't feel overly special.

85
00:07:15,017 --> 00:07:16,644
You guys going on a beer run?

86
00:07:16,727 --> 00:07:19,063
[man 3] You were always going
somewhere else.

87
00:07:19,147 --> 00:07:23,067
{\an8}Going to the city or going
to the Jersey Shore, going to Long Island.

88
00:07:23,151 --> 00:07:25,319
{\an8}And everybody was always
trying to get out.

89
00:07:25,403 --> 00:07:26,279
<i>♪ Yeah ♪</i>

90
00:07:26,362 --> 00:07:28,906
[school bell rings]

91
00:07:33,369 --> 00:07:35,371
[students chatter indistinctly]

92
00:07:37,623 --> 00:07:42,295
Hi, my name is Fred Isseks,
and I teach AV Graphics Communications.

93
00:07:42,378 --> 00:07:46,174
{\an8}It's a long name for a course
that involves electronic media.

94
00:07:46,257 --> 00:07:49,302
We hope you, uh, are intrigued
with the idea of taking this course,

95
00:07:49,385 --> 00:07:51,262
and that we see you in September.

96
00:07:51,345 --> 00:07:52,680
[serene music plays]

97
00:07:52,763 --> 00:07:56,058
[Jeff] You knew about Fred
before you met Fred.

98
00:07:57,602 --> 00:07:58,895
Like, when you get the schedules,

99
00:07:58,978 --> 00:08:00,813
they're like,
"You got Fred, you're so lucky."

100
00:08:00,897 --> 00:08:04,025
People used to call him
Hippie Fred or Crazy Fred.

101
00:08:04,108 --> 00:08:07,695
His hair, like, always got
a little long on top.

102
00:08:07,778 --> 00:08:10,656
[David] I do remember
very early on Fred just saying, like,

103
00:08:10,740 --> 00:08:12,950
"Yeah, one summer
I lived in the Grand Canyon."

104
00:08:14,035 --> 00:08:17,205
Other teachers sometimes would
kind of subtly denigrate him a little bit

105
00:08:17,288 --> 00:08:20,708
or suggest, you know, we're doing
serious stuff over in this classroom,

106
00:08:20,791 --> 00:08:23,503
but that guy does
that other stuff over there.

107
00:08:23,586 --> 00:08:27,381
Famous dead person to bring back,
it'd have to be Karl Marx.

108
00:08:27,465 --> 00:08:30,635
He could come back, and he could
check out all the predictions he made

109
00:08:30,718 --> 00:08:34,222
about late 20th century capitalism,
if they've come true or not.

110
00:08:35,223 --> 00:08:38,851
[David] He'd mention a philosopher,
or he'd mention Noam Chomsky,

111
00:08:38,935 --> 00:08:40,603
or he'd mention Ralph Nader.

112
00:08:40,686 --> 00:08:42,271
And I thought, "Wow, this guy's smart."

113
00:08:42,355 --> 00:08:45,233
{\an8}[Fred] I went to law school
for about two months.

114
00:08:45,316 --> 00:08:47,193
{\an8}I didn't even make it
to Thanksgiving vacation,

115
00:08:47,276 --> 00:08:49,320
{\an8}and I realized, "This is not for me."

116
00:08:50,154 --> 00:08:53,741
When I started teaching,
I fell in love with it immediately.

117
00:08:53,824 --> 00:08:57,870
I'm teaching ninth grade English,
<i>Romeo and Juliet, Of Mice and Men,</i>

118
00:08:57,954 --> 00:08:59,539
<i>The Old Man and the Sea.</i>

119
00:08:59,622 --> 00:09:02,750
But after a few years,
it was getting a little old to me,

120
00:09:02,833 --> 00:09:05,419
so I tried doing some extra little things.

121
00:09:06,462 --> 00:09:11,133
{\an8}I came up with this idea to apply
for a grant for all of $300

122
00:09:11,842 --> 00:09:14,178
{\an8}and get Instamatic cameras,

123
00:09:14,262 --> 00:09:17,390
{\an8}and give them to the students
and have them go out

124
00:09:17,473 --> 00:09:19,392
{\an8}and take pictures in town.

125
00:09:20,142 --> 00:09:23,062
{\an8}So when the district did get TV cameras,

126
00:09:23,145 --> 00:09:26,899
and when we did start to move into that,
they picked me to do it,

127
00:09:26,983 --> 00:09:30,444
because they said, "He knows how to use
a camera. He does the Instamatic."

128
00:09:31,195 --> 00:09:33,114
[director] Did you know
how to use those cameras?

129
00:09:33,197 --> 00:09:34,156
No.

130
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:36,242
[hopeful ethereal music plays]

131
00:09:43,666 --> 00:09:46,877
It's a vision of education
in the old model of John Dewey.

132
00:09:47,795 --> 00:09:51,340
You don't learn by sitting
in a classroom and taking notes.

133
00:09:52,675 --> 00:09:54,260
You learn by doing.

134
00:09:55,428 --> 00:09:57,680
You learn by getting involved.

135
00:10:05,730 --> 00:10:07,690
This is the TV studio.

136
00:10:09,567 --> 00:10:11,652
Essentially, we had
a control room in the back,

137
00:10:11,736 --> 00:10:14,697
and then this was our stage area.

138
00:10:14,780 --> 00:10:18,909
We had a round stage
that someone donated to us,

139
00:10:18,993 --> 00:10:21,329
and we had a backdrop
that was kind of flimsy.

140
00:10:21,412 --> 00:10:25,958
We had a couple of different
editing stations set up around the room

141
00:10:26,042 --> 00:10:27,960
where students would edit their footage.

142
00:10:28,753 --> 00:10:32,256
We had a public access station
a mile away from the school,

143
00:10:32,340 --> 00:10:35,176
and we could broadcast anytime we wanted.

144
00:10:37,178 --> 00:10:40,431
So I went from teaching 9th Grade English

145
00:10:40,514 --> 00:10:43,893
into doing an elective for seniors
called Electronic English.

146
00:10:43,976 --> 00:10:46,937
It was regular English,
except, uh, we plugged it in.

147
00:10:57,406 --> 00:10:58,699
Oh, man.

148
00:10:59,533 --> 00:11:00,910
Oh, whoa.

149
00:11:02,203 --> 00:11:04,205
[laughs fondly]

150
00:11:06,082 --> 00:11:07,416
It's amazing.

151
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:09,418
[Rachel laughs]

152
00:11:12,588 --> 00:11:13,756
Wow.

153
00:11:17,885 --> 00:11:19,011
[chuckles]

154
00:11:19,095 --> 00:11:20,971
It's going back in time.

155
00:11:25,685 --> 00:11:28,229
[director] Do you remember
what attracted you to Electronic English

156
00:11:28,312 --> 00:11:29,355
in the first place?

157
00:11:29,438 --> 00:11:32,066
I was cutting class.
I was in the parking lot.

158
00:11:32,149 --> 00:11:36,070
I was walking to Burger King,
and Fred saw me.

159
00:11:36,153 --> 00:11:38,739
And he said, "Look,
I have this class, Electronic English."

160
00:11:38,823 --> 00:11:40,866
"If you come to class,
I won't call your mom."

161
00:11:40,950 --> 00:11:42,076
And it just seemed fun.

162
00:11:42,910 --> 00:11:47,289
I watched a lot of TV as a kid,
and so I was always enamored by it.

163
00:11:48,666 --> 00:11:51,877
[David] The moment I walked in there,
it was just like this whole portal

164
00:11:51,961 --> 00:11:53,170
to a new world opened.

165
00:11:53,671 --> 00:11:58,050
There'd be kids in the corner
playing Onyx or, uh, Wu-Tang Clan

166
00:11:58,134 --> 00:11:59,510
and making a music video.

167
00:12:00,386 --> 00:12:03,305
But then right next to them
would be some hippie kids.

168
00:12:03,389 --> 00:12:05,057
It was kind of a free-for-all.

169
00:12:05,141 --> 00:12:06,767
[teenagers laugh]

170
00:12:06,851 --> 00:12:09,854
I just told the kids to be creative.
Use your talent.

171
00:12:09,937 --> 00:12:13,149
Use it to make stuff
instead of consuming stuff.

172
00:12:13,232 --> 00:12:15,234
[drum machine plays]

173
00:12:17,111 --> 00:12:18,863
[Mike] It's easy to look back and say,

174
00:12:18,946 --> 00:12:21,532
"Well, you just showed up and hung out
and he handed you a camera."

175
00:12:21,615 --> 00:12:22,992
But that's literally what happened.

176
00:12:23,576 --> 00:12:25,536
[Rachel] He let us shoot
rap videos in the hallway.

177
00:12:25,619 --> 00:12:28,164
-[sighs]
-[Rachel] Karate films.

178
00:12:29,498 --> 00:12:32,460
[teenager] ♪ <i>Look at yourself</i>
<i>You're on top of the world ♪</i>

179
00:12:32,543 --> 00:12:34,503
[Rachel] We were always
filming something funny.

180
00:12:34,587 --> 00:12:37,047
♪ <i>Sometimes you walk around</i>
<i>With your nose in the air </i>♪

181
00:12:37,131 --> 00:12:39,341
<i>♪ Other times you walk around</i>
<i>Like you just don't care ♪</i>

182
00:12:39,425 --> 00:12:42,678
<i>-♪ 'Cause you're conceited ♪</i>
<i>-♪ Yo-yo-yo-you're conceited ♪</i>

183
00:12:42,762 --> 00:12:44,221
[sampler continues]

184
00:12:44,305 --> 00:12:46,474
[David] Back then,
a camcorder was still a big deal.

185
00:12:46,557 --> 00:12:47,641
Hi!

186
00:12:49,351 --> 00:12:51,604
This thing that we associated
with Hollywood or something,

187
00:12:51,687 --> 00:12:52,938
so it was very exciting.

188
00:12:55,858 --> 00:12:59,153
Somewhere, there's
a very embarrassing video

189
00:12:59,236 --> 00:13:02,031
of me lip-syncing
to George Michael's "Faith."

190
00:13:02,114 --> 00:13:04,950
If I find that tape,
you will not see it. [laughs]

191
00:13:05,034 --> 00:13:06,786
[drum machine continues playing]

192
00:13:09,163 --> 00:13:10,164
[past director] Cut.

193
00:13:15,669 --> 00:13:20,090
[Jeff] Everybody had gotten their time
to play around with the equipment,

194
00:13:20,174 --> 00:13:22,885
but then Fred gathered everybody and said

195
00:13:22,968 --> 00:13:25,471
that we needed to talk
about an actual assignment.

196
00:13:26,055 --> 00:13:28,599
[Fred] The school district left it
pretty much open to me

197
00:13:28,682 --> 00:13:30,184
how I wanted to structure it.

198
00:13:30,267 --> 00:13:32,520
But it had to be something
having to do with English.

199
00:13:32,603 --> 00:13:33,562
[singing]

200
00:13:33,646 --> 00:13:35,940
I could have taken it
in a different direction.

201
00:13:36,023 --> 00:13:37,691
We could have produced plays.

202
00:13:37,775 --> 00:13:41,111
But for some reason,
that didn't really occur to me.

203
00:13:41,195 --> 00:13:45,658
What… kind of resonated with me
was the idea of doing journalism.

204
00:13:45,741 --> 00:13:46,659
[marching band plays]

205
00:13:46,742 --> 00:13:49,286
Let's investigate all kinds of stories.

206
00:13:49,370 --> 00:13:51,872
Let's tell the story of the cheerleaders,

207
00:13:51,956 --> 00:13:54,583
the football team, and the Home Ec class.

208
00:13:54,667 --> 00:13:56,460
[all cheering]

209
00:13:56,544 --> 00:13:58,546
While all that's going on,

210
00:13:58,629 --> 00:14:01,924
I'm gonna turn you on to a story
that I think is pretty incredible,

211
00:14:02,007 --> 00:14:04,051
and you're more than welcome
to jump in on it.

212
00:14:06,929 --> 00:14:09,306
Fred's like, "Yeah,
there's this thing in the landfill."

213
00:14:09,390 --> 00:14:12,059
And we're like, "Yeah, whatever."
We really didn't care.

214
00:14:13,185 --> 00:14:16,355
But he was like, "Stop what you're doing.
All right, check this out."

215
00:14:16,438 --> 00:14:17,773
[gloomy ethereal music plays]

216
00:14:17,857 --> 00:14:24,154
[woman] Last year, I noticed fluid
coming out of the middle of the road.

217
00:14:24,238 --> 00:14:28,701
And one day I got out of my car,
I touched the fluid,

218
00:14:28,784 --> 00:14:32,997
and what I would think
would have been oil on the road

219
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:37,459
was a total spot of brown water.

220
00:14:38,085 --> 00:14:41,672
And I was told by someone in the village

221
00:14:41,755 --> 00:14:45,175
that it was from the storm sewer.

222
00:14:45,676 --> 00:14:49,013
I could go across the street
and ask my neighbor for a glass of water,

223
00:14:49,096 --> 00:14:50,472
and I could put it up to my--

224
00:14:50,556 --> 00:14:53,976
The minute she turns on the tap,
the whole house stinks.

225
00:14:54,059 --> 00:14:56,061
I mean, it's raunchy smelling.

226
00:14:58,731 --> 00:15:01,859
[Fred] I had an old friend
named Don Tirrell.

227
00:15:02,359 --> 00:15:04,904
He lived out on a farm that he had bought.

228
00:15:05,696 --> 00:15:07,907
He said, "We've got a mess
in our backyards

229
00:15:07,990 --> 00:15:09,158
that we didn't know about."

230
00:15:10,284 --> 00:15:13,370
[Jeff] He thought
he was getting this bucolic life,

231
00:15:13,454 --> 00:15:17,625
and then he ended up living
so close to a toxic landfill.

232
00:15:17,708 --> 00:15:19,418
-Is this on right now?
-[past Fred] Yup.

233
00:15:20,419 --> 00:15:23,797
We were interviewing Don's neighbors.
I worked the camera,

234
00:15:23,881 --> 00:15:25,257
and Don asked the questions.

235
00:15:25,341 --> 00:15:29,470
Right now, I am at breaking point.
I really cannot take it much longer.

236
00:15:29,553 --> 00:15:32,556
Wonderful shot. Did you get that?
Could you do that again?

237
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:36,143
[woman] And look at this. This--
This has got to be a health hazard also.

238
00:15:36,226 --> 00:15:37,728
This is draining right into my yard.

239
00:15:37,811 --> 00:15:40,356
Right now, if I cut my grass,
I've got to go this way.

240
00:15:40,439 --> 00:15:42,316
So eventually, what's going to be here?

241
00:15:42,399 --> 00:15:45,319
-All this… running out.
-And where does it go?

242
00:15:49,323 --> 00:15:51,283
-[woman] How's Jane doing? Okay?
-[Don] Yeah, good.

243
00:15:51,367 --> 00:15:53,452
[woman] I did enjoy that thing
at your house Sunday.

244
00:15:53,535 --> 00:15:55,579
-It was very nice.
-[Don] Thank you.

245
00:15:55,663 --> 00:15:56,830
Unbelievable.

246
00:15:56,914 --> 00:15:58,165
[woman] Isn't it?

247
00:15:58,248 --> 00:15:59,291
See? It's like an orange.

248
00:15:59,375 --> 00:16:01,752
-[Fred] Come in on it.
-[woman] What is that thing?

249
00:16:05,005 --> 00:16:08,008
[Don] What do you think, Fred?
Think she's got a problem?

250
00:16:08,550 --> 00:16:11,971
[past Fred] I wouldn't want to build
a family room down here.

251
00:16:12,054 --> 00:16:14,306
[woman] Every morning
before leaving for work,

252
00:16:14,390 --> 00:16:16,642
I start with this one,
go down to sump pump.

253
00:16:16,725 --> 00:16:18,060
Drain out.

254
00:16:19,645 --> 00:16:21,647
[woman continues splashing water on TV]

255
00:16:31,782 --> 00:16:34,994
[past Fred] Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa!
Folks, if you can…

256
00:16:35,077 --> 00:16:36,286
[students chatter]

257
00:16:36,370 --> 00:16:38,831
If you guys can hold off for just another…

258
00:16:38,914 --> 00:16:40,582
Excuse me.

259
00:16:41,625 --> 00:16:44,920
If you guys can hold off
for just another three minutes,

260
00:16:45,004 --> 00:16:46,088
we'll be done.

261
00:16:47,172 --> 00:16:50,217
[Fred] I said, you know, "This is
a story we can look into further."

262
00:16:50,300 --> 00:16:54,054
Well, there's a type of student that…
[mimics whooshing] they woke right up.

263
00:16:55,264 --> 00:16:58,142
[Rachel] Once Fred started
explaining the story

264
00:16:58,225 --> 00:17:02,062
in the way, like,
rebellious teenagers could understand,

265
00:17:02,146 --> 00:17:04,732
then that, to me, was really exciting.

266
00:17:04,815 --> 00:17:05,733
[gentle music plays]

267
00:17:05,816 --> 00:17:07,693
[man] And who do we find here?

268
00:17:08,610 --> 00:17:13,115
Zoom in.
Oh, there's your heartthrob, Corey Haim.

269
00:17:13,198 --> 00:17:15,659
You're recording, you stupid shit.

270
00:17:16,160 --> 00:17:18,203
[Rachel] I wasn't into politics.

271
00:17:18,287 --> 00:17:20,581
I wasn't an environmentalist.

272
00:17:22,499 --> 00:17:25,169
That was the opposite of my vibe.

273
00:17:25,252 --> 00:17:26,712
Press the button.

274
00:17:27,921 --> 00:17:29,256
[man] Who's that?

275
00:17:29,339 --> 00:17:31,550
[past Rachel] David Lynch. He is cool.

276
00:17:31,633 --> 00:17:33,635
Didn't you see <i>Wild at Heart?</i>

277
00:17:34,762 --> 00:17:36,221
[Rachel] Because I was Puerto Rican,

278
00:17:36,305 --> 00:17:38,974
I was, like, good
with all the Puerto Rican kids.

279
00:17:40,184 --> 00:17:42,853
I also had a bunch of goth friends.

280
00:17:42,936 --> 00:17:47,191
We would hop on the train
to go into the city to go to nightclubs.

281
00:17:47,983 --> 00:17:52,946
I loved any kind of, like, punk rock,
in-your-face music.

282
00:17:53,030 --> 00:17:55,991
Anything that was
sort of against the system.

283
00:17:57,242 --> 00:18:00,537
But deep down, I was cripplingly shy.

284
00:18:03,123 --> 00:18:05,417
-[student] What do we have here?
-I'm yours!

285
00:18:05,501 --> 00:18:06,877
[teenagers chatter]

286
00:18:08,670 --> 00:18:09,713
[students exclaim]

287
00:18:09,797 --> 00:18:11,840
[Rachel] Here we go.
Get in the car. [laughs]

288
00:18:13,759 --> 00:18:16,345
[Jeff] I think I was always
a-- a bit of a chameleon.

289
00:18:16,845 --> 00:18:21,475
I could kind of hang out with the jocks,
uh, because I played football.

290
00:18:21,558 --> 00:18:25,354
I could hang out with the stoners
because I also smoked pot.

291
00:18:25,437 --> 00:18:27,439
["Jump Around" by House of Pain
plays on car stereo]

292
00:18:27,523 --> 00:18:29,858
<i>♪ Pack it up, pack it in</i>
<i>Let me begin… ♪</i>

293
00:18:29,942 --> 00:18:34,071
But I-- Probably back in the '90s,
they would have called me a poseur.

294
00:18:34,780 --> 00:18:35,697
I don't know.

295
00:18:36,448 --> 00:18:38,867
I could have been an asshole. [laughs]

296
00:18:38,951 --> 00:18:40,911
[cadets shout in unison]

297
00:18:41,495 --> 00:18:43,914
[Mike] My core group was the ROTC kids.

298
00:18:45,165 --> 00:18:47,584
We march around with rifles,
we're spinning and flipping them.

299
00:18:50,379 --> 00:18:52,881
My father left
when I was about 4 years old,

300
00:18:52,965 --> 00:18:55,592
and my stepfather's a Marine,
he was tough.

301
00:18:55,676 --> 00:18:58,595
So I was left on my own,
like a typical latchkey kid,

302
00:18:58,679 --> 00:19:00,722
to figure things out.

303
00:19:02,516 --> 00:19:05,144
I mean, technically,
I wasn't even in Fred's class.

304
00:19:05,727 --> 00:19:09,148
He just let me hang around and participate
because he took a shine to me.

305
00:19:12,734 --> 00:19:15,529
[David] I think
I was maybe an outsider.

306
00:19:16,613 --> 00:19:17,656
I had a lot of friends,

307
00:19:17,739 --> 00:19:21,243
but I never felt
like I was in a clique per se.

308
00:19:22,244 --> 00:19:25,998
I had always been involved in some, like,
youth and government political things.

309
00:19:26,081 --> 00:19:30,127
But the truth is I wanted to, like,
blow up every corporate headquarters.

310
00:19:30,210 --> 00:19:31,545
<i>I do believe it's a health hazard.</i>

311
00:19:31,628 --> 00:19:35,007
<i>My one daughter, my older one,</i>
<i>had suffered a lot with strep throats.</i>

312
00:19:35,090 --> 00:19:38,594
[David] So when I saw
this footage, I was hooked.

313
00:19:38,677 --> 00:19:40,053
[woman] <i>And the doctor could never…</i>

314
00:19:40,137 --> 00:19:41,847
[David] And I was like, "Oh!"

315
00:19:41,930 --> 00:19:45,309
Like, "Okay." So--
So this is bigger than school was.

316
00:19:45,809 --> 00:19:49,188
I wanted to talk to anybody
that knew anything about this.

317
00:19:49,771 --> 00:19:53,275
How many people are gonna be available
to go to the landfill?

318
00:19:53,775 --> 00:19:57,196
[Jeff] It wasn't presented
as a class requirement to do this.

319
00:19:57,279 --> 00:19:58,989
It was more like a soft pitch.

320
00:19:59,072 --> 00:20:01,658
Brian, Jen, Miguel…

321
00:20:01,742 --> 00:20:04,953
It was just like, "Hey, we're going
to the dump. Anybody want to come?"

322
00:20:05,037 --> 00:20:08,248
People would sort of say, like,
you know, scratch their heads, I think,

323
00:20:08,332 --> 00:20:10,083
and go, "You're going to the dump?"

324
00:20:10,167 --> 00:20:13,003
[Rachel] "Mom, can I go?" They're like,
"Are you going with your teacher?"

325
00:20:13,086 --> 00:20:15,923
I'm like, "Yes."
[boisterously] "Sure, you could go."

326
00:20:16,673 --> 00:20:18,675
[mysterious music plays]

327
00:20:25,849 --> 00:20:28,477
[past Mike] I don't know
where we're going. Down some dirt road.

328
00:20:29,061 --> 00:20:31,021
There's, like, garbage everywhere.

329
00:20:31,104 --> 00:20:33,232
It's a dump, yeah.
What do I expect, right?

330
00:20:45,327 --> 00:20:47,579
[Fred] People said,
"Hey, you're being careful, right?"

331
00:20:47,663 --> 00:20:49,539
And I'd say, "Yes, I am."

332
00:20:50,958 --> 00:20:52,709
[director] Did you trespass?

333
00:20:53,794 --> 00:20:54,670
Yes.

334
00:20:58,674 --> 00:21:01,051
[Rachel] It was trespassing.
I was breaking the law.

335
00:21:01,635 --> 00:21:05,097
I climbed over a fence,
and someone handed me a camera.

336
00:21:05,639 --> 00:21:06,807
-[boy] Hey, Rachel.
-What?

337
00:21:06,890 --> 00:21:07,975
[boy] Smile for us.

338
00:21:11,103 --> 00:21:12,854
[teenagers chatter indistinctly]

339
00:21:24,700 --> 00:21:27,619
[past Rachel] Jeff, is it taping
when the arrows are moving

340
00:21:27,703 --> 00:21:28,996
or when it's stuck?

341
00:21:29,079 --> 00:21:30,872
-When the arrows are moving.
-All right.

342
00:21:32,082 --> 00:21:33,041
What are you taping?

343
00:21:33,125 --> 00:21:34,459
[Fred] Where did that come from?

344
00:21:34,543 --> 00:21:35,711
[Jeff] All the holes.

345
00:21:35,794 --> 00:21:37,129
[chuckles]

346
00:21:37,629 --> 00:21:39,548
There was
some pretty good camera work in there.

347
00:21:39,631 --> 00:21:42,467
Maybe some of my finest
early camera work. [laughs]

348
00:21:42,551 --> 00:21:44,803
[Jeff] Someone should lie down in it
and just act dead.

349
00:21:44,886 --> 00:21:47,389
-[Mike] Yeah, you lie in this shit.
-[Jeff laughs]

350
00:21:52,769 --> 00:21:54,354
[grunts]

351
00:21:56,148 --> 00:21:57,733
[Rachel] Jeff, get up here!

352
00:21:58,567 --> 00:22:00,027
[Jeff laughs]

353
00:22:00,110 --> 00:22:01,987
[sarcastically] All right,
honey, I'm coming.

354
00:22:06,283 --> 00:22:08,577
[Fred] Don't go over the edge.
They'll see us.

355
00:22:09,578 --> 00:22:11,246
[Jeff] Get a shot of that.

356
00:22:13,206 --> 00:22:15,083
[Rachel] It's like bubbling and shit.

357
00:22:15,167 --> 00:22:17,252
[teenager] Get a lighter.
Who's got a lighter?

358
00:22:17,336 --> 00:22:19,338
-[Fred] I wouldn't.
-[Jeff laughs]

359
00:22:19,421 --> 00:22:20,339
[bubbling]

360
00:22:20,422 --> 00:22:22,924
-[teenager 1] You hear it?
-[teenager 2] Yeah. I can hear it.

361
00:22:23,508 --> 00:22:24,343
[Fred] Hear what?

362
00:22:24,426 --> 00:22:25,844
-Rach.
-[Rachel] Hmm?

363
00:22:25,927 --> 00:22:28,597
[boy] Hear that. Get over here, get sound.
Where is that coming from?

364
00:22:28,680 --> 00:22:30,098
[Jeff] Right in that mound of dirt.

365
00:22:31,475 --> 00:22:32,684
Get the camera right close.

366
00:22:32,768 --> 00:22:34,644
-[Rachel] Where is it?
-[Fred] Hey, here you go.

367
00:22:34,728 --> 00:22:37,147
-[David] What the hell is that?
-[Fred] Just listen.

368
00:22:37,230 --> 00:22:39,649
[Rachel] Oh, God, it smells like shit!

369
00:22:39,733 --> 00:22:41,318
-[teenagers laugh]
-Damn!

370
00:22:41,943 --> 00:22:43,945
[unsettling ethereal music plays]

371
00:22:45,030 --> 00:22:48,700
We're on the edge
of, uh, some kind of pond or pool.

372
00:22:48,784 --> 00:22:52,329
There's a green tint to it.

373
00:22:52,829 --> 00:22:54,498
[director] How would you
describe the smell?

374
00:22:54,581 --> 00:22:56,500
Oh, dead bodies and decay.

375
00:22:56,583 --> 00:22:58,460
Um, New Jersey.

376
00:22:59,586 --> 00:23:01,421
[past Mike] This is me skipping stones.

377
00:23:09,179 --> 00:23:11,264
[Rachel] We did step carefully,

378
00:23:11,765 --> 00:23:15,268
but it feels like
there was a little stupidity

379
00:23:15,352 --> 00:23:18,605
meets, like, being naive.

380
00:23:21,274 --> 00:23:24,820
As high school students,
we just saw bits and pieces.

381
00:23:25,695 --> 00:23:29,991
I don't think any of us
had any sense of the scale or depth

382
00:23:30,075 --> 00:23:31,868
of the story yet.

383
00:23:34,913 --> 00:23:39,084
{\an8}[reporter] <i>Shakopee, Minnesota,</i>
<i>outside Minneapolis, 1973.</i>

384
00:23:40,877 --> 00:23:44,673
<i>A spectacular fire burns</i>
<i>4,000 waste drums,</i>

385
00:23:44,756 --> 00:23:49,469
<i>sending noxious clouds of smoke</i>
<i>and some of the barrels into the sky.</i>

386
00:23:50,262 --> 00:23:53,557
<i>Leakage from this dump site</i>
<i>has now poisoned groundwater here</i>

387
00:23:53,640 --> 00:23:55,475
<i>with arsenic and lead.</i>

388
00:23:57,060 --> 00:23:59,980
[Fred] Though there were other stories
that were percolating around then

389
00:24:00,063 --> 00:24:03,150
about landfills and the problems
that they caused,

390
00:24:03,233 --> 00:24:05,944
Love Canal was the granddaddy of them all.

391
00:24:06,611 --> 00:24:10,157
{\an8}[reporter] <i>Niagara Falls, New York,</i>
<i>August 1978.</i>

392
00:24:10,782 --> 00:24:13,743
{\an8}<i>Deadly chemicals seeping out</i>
<i>of an abandoned waste dump</i>

393
00:24:13,827 --> 00:24:17,122
<i>called the Love Canal</i>
<i>are blamed for health problems</i>

394
00:24:17,205 --> 00:24:20,208
<i>ranging from nervous disorders</i>
<i>to birth defects.</i>

395
00:24:20,292 --> 00:24:24,212
<i>Five-year-old Mark Dunmire lives</i>
<i>five blocks from the canal.</i>

396
00:24:24,296 --> 00:24:26,214
<i>He was born with a heart murmur,</i>

397
00:24:26,298 --> 00:24:29,885
<i>a urinary tract obstruction,</i>
<i>and a diseased pancreas.</i>

398
00:24:29,968 --> 00:24:32,304
<i>He can't play in his basement anymore.</i>

399
00:24:32,387 --> 00:24:33,722
<i>It's contaminated.</i>

400
00:24:33,805 --> 00:24:35,348
[interviewer] What's in there?

401
00:24:36,141 --> 00:24:37,434
Chemicals.

402
00:24:38,143 --> 00:24:40,395
And what happens with those chemicals?

403
00:24:40,479 --> 00:24:42,439
They get you very sick.

404
00:24:44,691 --> 00:24:48,236
I'm happy to say, however, that based
on what we have seen and evaluated,

405
00:24:48,320 --> 00:24:51,698
there is no evidence of benzene toxicity.

406
00:24:51,781 --> 00:24:54,618
We are coming across some abnormalities.

407
00:24:54,701 --> 00:24:56,578
This is to be expected in any population.

408
00:24:56,661 --> 00:24:59,539
As you know, there are a variety of things
that can cause liver disease

409
00:24:59,623 --> 00:25:00,957
besides toxic chemicals.

410
00:25:01,541 --> 00:25:02,626
[moderator] Yes, ma'am.

411
00:25:02,709 --> 00:25:04,836
[woman] I have
an eight-and-a-half-year-old asthmatic.

412
00:25:04,920 --> 00:25:08,381
I have been told by my doctors
to get her out of this area.

413
00:25:08,465 --> 00:25:09,299
Perhaps under…

414
00:25:09,382 --> 00:25:10,759
[Fred] It was a travesty.

415
00:25:11,343 --> 00:25:15,931
[desperately] Would you please tell me,
do I let my 3-year-old stay?

416
00:25:16,515 --> 00:25:19,476
She has a birth defect now.

417
00:25:19,559 --> 00:25:23,146
What are you gonna do for my kids?
What are you gonna do? Nothing!

418
00:25:23,230 --> 00:25:24,439
[man] They gotta go.

419
00:25:24,523 --> 00:25:27,359
It-- The damage is done, man!
The damage is done!

420
00:25:27,943 --> 00:25:30,779
[students chatter indistinctly]

421
00:25:30,862 --> 00:25:35,325
[Fred] I believe that what was happening
near the Wallkill Dump was the same thing.

422
00:25:36,910 --> 00:25:40,038
This is like Love Canal.
This is as bad as Love Canal.

423
00:25:40,789 --> 00:25:41,915
And Don agreed.

424
00:25:41,998 --> 00:25:43,333
[test tone plays]

425
00:25:43,416 --> 00:25:44,376
[Don clears throat]

426
00:25:44,459 --> 00:25:46,461
[studio audience chatters indistinctly]

427
00:25:53,885 --> 00:25:55,845
Good morning, my name is Valerie Basso,

428
00:25:55,929 --> 00:25:58,056
and I'm a student
at Middletown High School,

429
00:25:58,139 --> 00:26:00,642
and I am here interviewing Don.

430
00:26:00,725 --> 00:26:02,644
Thank you for coming, everybody.

431
00:26:02,727 --> 00:26:07,274
Don, how do you feel about this landfill
and everything, about being around it?

432
00:26:08,191 --> 00:26:12,237
Uh, well, it's taken a long time
to even-- to find any feelings

433
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:15,240
{\an8}because when I first got involved,
I didn't know what the situation was.

434
00:26:15,323 --> 00:26:18,159
{\an8}I didn't know how bad the toxic waste was.

435
00:26:19,035 --> 00:26:20,120
Something isn't right.

436
00:26:20,203 --> 00:26:23,915
There's a feeling in the community
of something not right.

437
00:26:24,457 --> 00:26:27,502
[Valerie] Do you think eventually
the water will cause birth defects, or…?

438
00:26:27,586 --> 00:26:30,630
-It is already. It has to.
-[audience agrees]

439
00:26:30,714 --> 00:26:34,759
One woman came up in tears.
She had three children who were deaf.

440
00:26:35,969 --> 00:26:37,929
And she swears the water did it.

441
00:26:38,972 --> 00:26:41,474
But all I've ever done
since I ever came here was fight.

442
00:26:42,267 --> 00:26:43,852
And I think that people like yourselves,

443
00:26:43,935 --> 00:26:46,187
I think your generation
are willing to fight.

444
00:26:47,272 --> 00:26:50,025
[boy] But what makes you think
that the politicians and everybody

445
00:26:50,108 --> 00:26:52,736
are gonna be willing to listen?
How do you think we get through?

446
00:26:52,819 --> 00:26:56,239
You get on national television,
on Mike Wallace,

447
00:26:56,323 --> 00:26:57,949
you're gonna make the great changes.

448
00:26:58,033 --> 00:27:00,201
I don't believe that. I know that.

449
00:27:01,036 --> 00:27:04,748
Um, if we wanna find out more about this,
who do we invite in for an interview?

450
00:27:04,831 --> 00:27:06,041
[Don] Everyone.

451
00:27:06,833 --> 00:27:09,628
Everyone has a little piece
of the-- of the story.

452
00:27:09,711 --> 00:27:12,714
That's why film is a wonderful medium.

453
00:27:12,797 --> 00:27:16,384
It is our literature
for the next thousand years.

454
00:27:16,468 --> 00:27:18,094
[suspenseful music plays]

455
00:27:18,178 --> 00:27:20,180
[teenagers chatter indistinctly]

456
00:27:21,931 --> 00:27:24,559
-[boy] The smell is making me sick, Fred.
-[Fred] Yeah?

457
00:27:24,643 --> 00:27:26,811
[boy] Yeah, I don't feel well.

458
00:27:26,895 --> 00:27:28,647
-[Fred] You serious? Me too.
-Yeah!

459
00:27:29,356 --> 00:27:30,857
Give us a line, D.B.

460
00:27:30,940 --> 00:27:31,900
Beyotch.

461
00:27:31,983 --> 00:27:35,153
[laughs] I can't believe
you guys have this tape.

462
00:27:36,237 --> 00:27:38,698
Get your establishing shot, Trey.

463
00:27:43,370 --> 00:27:46,247
[Fred] You know, you want
to expose students to stuff.

464
00:27:46,331 --> 00:27:48,416
Here it is. This is real life.

465
00:27:49,542 --> 00:27:51,378
We weren't doing <i>Romeo and Juliet,</i>

466
00:27:51,461 --> 00:27:53,421
but we were doing Dutch and Willy.

467
00:27:53,505 --> 00:27:55,340
Now, is your last name Meyer?

468
00:27:55,423 --> 00:27:57,676
-Myers. M-Y-E-R-S.
-Myers, Willy.

469
00:27:57,759 --> 00:27:58,802
Bill Myers.

470
00:27:58,885 --> 00:28:02,138
{\an8}I'm here with Dutch Smith,
former employee of the Wallkill Landfill.

471
00:28:02,222 --> 00:28:03,056
{\an8}[coughs]

472
00:28:03,139 --> 00:28:04,891
{\an8}How long did you work at the landfill?

473
00:28:04,974 --> 00:28:06,851
Dutch and Willy were such characters.

474
00:28:07,519 --> 00:28:10,230
I feel like if you went to Central Casting

475
00:28:10,313 --> 00:28:12,649
and asked for a landfill worker,

476
00:28:12,732 --> 00:28:14,067
you'd get Dutch Smith.

477
00:28:14,567 --> 00:28:16,611
He kind of reminded me of Popeye.

478
00:28:17,737 --> 00:28:20,824
[Mike] I mean, Dutch still lived
in a trailer right next to the dump.

479
00:28:20,907 --> 00:28:22,117
[Fred laughs]

480
00:28:22,200 --> 00:28:23,868
You knew they lived there
their whole life.

481
00:28:23,952 --> 00:28:26,579
You knew they knew everybody.
Everybody knew them.

482
00:28:26,663 --> 00:28:30,458
You-- You just knew guys like that
weren't making stuff up.

483
00:28:30,542 --> 00:28:32,293
[Jeff] What have you seen
in your years here?

484
00:28:32,377 --> 00:28:35,213
Everything. If you can name it,
it's in there.

485
00:28:35,296 --> 00:28:37,716
-[Jeff] And how much went in?
-Plenty.

486
00:28:37,799 --> 00:28:40,218
-Every day?
-Every single day of the week.

487
00:28:40,969 --> 00:28:42,721
Some of it was like jelly.

488
00:28:44,013 --> 00:28:46,266
Some of it was in a powder form.

489
00:28:46,766 --> 00:28:50,562
Some of it was in like a-- a cube form.

490
00:28:50,645 --> 00:28:54,023
There's thousands and thousands
of tons of toxic waste in there.

491
00:28:54,107 --> 00:28:56,860
And I know it's in there
because I buried lots of it.

492
00:28:56,943 --> 00:29:00,363
Well, mostly it was me
and, uh, Dutch's father-in-law

493
00:29:00,447 --> 00:29:01,865
running the dump.

494
00:29:01,948 --> 00:29:03,700
And then he got in that problem.

495
00:29:04,200 --> 00:29:05,869
-[Don] What happened to him?
-Well, he--

496
00:29:05,952 --> 00:29:08,163
-Were you there when it happened?
-Yeah, I was there.

497
00:29:08,246 --> 00:29:10,498
He run into the powder chemicals.

498
00:29:10,999 --> 00:29:14,127
And the cab all filled up
and, you know, and, uh…

499
00:29:15,420 --> 00:29:18,256
He couldn't breathe very good.
They took him to the hospital.

500
00:29:18,339 --> 00:29:21,676
He was in bad shape before he died.
Couldn't get his breath.

501
00:29:22,802 --> 00:29:26,139
To go from Don and Fred
kind of talking about it,

502
00:29:26,222 --> 00:29:30,101
once you have
these firsthand accounts of it,

503
00:29:30,185 --> 00:29:31,394
it becomes real.

504
00:29:31,895 --> 00:29:35,607
Anybody come in there without a permit,
we used to tell them you couldn't dump.

505
00:29:35,690 --> 00:29:36,608
[Don] Right.

506
00:29:36,691 --> 00:29:39,360
Ten minutes later or a half hour later,
they'd be back.

507
00:29:39,444 --> 00:29:41,029
They had a permit to dump.

508
00:29:41,112 --> 00:29:45,200
There was very few what you could call
local trucks around here.

509
00:29:45,283 --> 00:29:47,076
It-- It was all big companies.

510
00:29:47,160 --> 00:29:49,287
-[Don] From out of the area.
-From out of the area.

511
00:29:49,871 --> 00:29:52,457
Yeah, well, I knowed it was wrong
by putting it in there,

512
00:29:52,540 --> 00:29:55,919
{\an8}but, you know, hey, the town gave them
the okay and I worked for the town.

513
00:29:56,002 --> 00:29:58,630
What could I say?
Well, I couldn't tell the guy to leave.

514
00:29:58,713 --> 00:30:00,757
-All right, thanks a lot, Dutch.
-Yeah, be good.

515
00:30:02,050 --> 00:30:03,468
[David] I was so excited.

516
00:30:05,094 --> 00:30:07,305
We thought for sure
we had blown this thing open

517
00:30:07,388 --> 00:30:09,390
and we were going to make some headlines.

518
00:30:09,891 --> 00:30:12,435
<i>I told my wife, I said,</i>
<i>"They're all Mafia-owned."</i>

519
00:30:12,519 --> 00:30:13,645
<i>"I'll lay money on it."</i>

520
00:30:13,728 --> 00:30:15,897
[Fred] I felt like, "Wow, what a scoop."

521
00:30:15,980 --> 00:30:19,192
I mean, if the newspaper knew this,
the story was going to get out.

522
00:30:20,109 --> 00:30:24,781
The <i>Times Herald-Record </i>played
a vital role in the community.

523
00:30:26,241 --> 00:30:30,119
If it didn't make it into the <i>Record,</i>
it was almost as if it didn't happen.

524
00:30:31,412 --> 00:30:33,248
For a minute,
I thought you worked for Cable 6,

525
00:30:33,331 --> 00:30:34,749
and I noticed you were shooting VHS.

526
00:30:34,833 --> 00:30:36,459
[teenager] Yeah. Middletown High School.

527
00:30:36,543 --> 00:30:38,586
Yeah, okay. You work with Fred?

528
00:30:38,670 --> 00:30:40,922
We thought our local paper
would be excited and outraged

529
00:30:41,005 --> 00:30:42,590
over the information we brought to light,

530
00:30:42,674 --> 00:30:45,385
but we found out we got
no encouragement from them whatsoever.

531
00:30:46,052 --> 00:30:49,556
[David] So it was like,
okay, new approach.

532
00:30:49,639 --> 00:30:53,977
{\an8}Let's just invite the paper's editor
to come talk to our journalism class.

533
00:30:54,519 --> 00:30:56,479
I think he thought,
"Yeah, I'm just gonna go talk

534
00:30:56,563 --> 00:30:58,940
to a few kids in a high school."
How bad could that go?

535
00:30:59,023 --> 00:31:00,400
[monitor beeps]

536
00:31:03,862 --> 00:31:06,281
<i>Hi, I'm Trey Long here with Gary Grossman,</i>

537
00:31:06,364 --> 00:31:08,283
<i>the editor-in-chief</i>
<i>of the </i>Times Herald-Record.

538
00:31:08,366 --> 00:31:10,201
<i>Thank you very much</i>
<i>for coming, Mr. Grossman.</i>

539
00:31:10,285 --> 00:31:13,746
Um, one thing that we find ourselves
very concerned with is the environment.

540
00:31:13,830 --> 00:31:15,790
And I know Jeff has some questions for you

541
00:31:15,874 --> 00:31:19,043
concerning the environment
as far as Orange County

542
00:31:19,127 --> 00:31:21,004
and how the <i>Record </i>deals with it.

543
00:31:21,504 --> 00:31:23,506
Um… Um…

544
00:31:25,008 --> 00:31:27,510
<i>We, uh… A lot of students</i>
<i>in the journalism class,</i>

545
00:31:27,594 --> 00:31:30,680
<i>we made a video about solid waste</i>
<i>disposal in Orange County.</i>

546
00:31:30,763 --> 00:31:32,265
<i>And I dropped off the tape</i>

547
00:31:32,348 --> 00:31:34,601
<i>at the, um, </i>Times Herald-Record
<i>Middletown office.</i>

548
00:31:34,684 --> 00:31:36,311
{\an8}I was wondering if you ever saw that tape.

549
00:31:36,394 --> 00:31:38,062
{\an8}-No. No.
-You haven't.

550
00:31:38,146 --> 00:31:41,858
{\an8}Well, let me explain to you
a little about what it was. Um…

551
00:31:41,941 --> 00:31:45,403
[sniffs] It was about the-- the problem
we're having in the landfills

552
00:31:45,486 --> 00:31:48,281
and the problem with solid waste disposal.

553
00:31:48,364 --> 00:31:52,911
And one of the major differences
between our tape, the focus point,

554
00:31:52,994 --> 00:31:58,207
was that our tape showed it
as an environmental problem.

555
00:31:58,291 --> 00:32:00,084
{\an8}Whereas I think that,

556
00:32:00,168 --> 00:32:02,587
{\an8}from what I've read
in the reporting in <i>The Record,</i>

557
00:32:02,670 --> 00:32:06,049
{\an8}it sees it more as an economic problem
for the people of Orange County.

558
00:32:06,132 --> 00:32:07,675
{\an8}What do you think about that?

559
00:32:07,759 --> 00:32:10,678
Well, I think they're probably hard
to, uh, separate, don't you?

560
00:32:12,138 --> 00:32:15,308
-[Jeff] It'd be hard to separate?
-Yeah. I-- I think that, uh…

561
00:32:16,476 --> 00:32:19,771
a lot of environmental solutions
have economic costs.

562
00:32:19,854 --> 00:32:23,066
And to some extent, the determination
of the environmental solution…

563
00:32:24,776 --> 00:32:28,154
uh, depends on what people
are willing to pay for it.

564
00:32:28,237 --> 00:32:30,406
He looks so mad.

565
00:32:32,951 --> 00:32:34,744
{\an8}[past Jeff] Well, um, another difference

566
00:32:34,827 --> 00:32:37,455
is that a lot of people
that we've interviewed,

567
00:32:37,538 --> 00:32:40,249
um, blame the problem on corruption.

568
00:32:40,333 --> 00:32:42,835
-What was your question?
-I'm just saying, um…

569
00:32:43,419 --> 00:32:46,464
that the people
we've interviewed have said

570
00:32:46,547 --> 00:32:49,133
that there is a problem there
of corruption.

571
00:32:49,217 --> 00:32:51,594
Possibly. Do they have proof?

572
00:32:51,678 --> 00:32:52,637
[Jeff sniffs]

573
00:32:53,763 --> 00:32:55,348
Well, um…

574
00:32:56,057 --> 00:32:58,059
We're all entitled to our opinions
about everything.

575
00:32:58,142 --> 00:32:59,978
One of the things
we have to learn as reporters

576
00:33:00,061 --> 00:33:02,730
is that, uh…
There's an old saying in our business,

577
00:33:02,814 --> 00:33:05,024
when your mother says she loves you,
check it out.

578
00:33:06,442 --> 00:33:07,318
And that is that, uh,

579
00:33:07,402 --> 00:33:09,946
just about anything you see,
you should be skeptical about.

580
00:33:10,613 --> 00:33:12,907
Gary's mom didn't love him.

581
00:33:12,991 --> 00:33:16,119
<i>But don't you see that as corruption?</i>
<i>Isn't there a cover-up there?</i>

582
00:33:16,202 --> 00:33:18,204
I don't know. I mean, do you?

583
00:33:18,287 --> 00:33:20,498
-Yeah, I see it as corruption.
-I don't know. Okay.

584
00:33:20,581 --> 00:33:22,583
Well, you're entitled to your opinion.

585
00:33:23,334 --> 00:33:26,087
The point I made earlier,
which I don't think you quite get,

586
00:33:26,170 --> 00:33:28,214
is that beliefs are not important.

587
00:33:28,297 --> 00:33:30,883
Verification is important.
Reporting is important.

588
00:33:30,967 --> 00:33:33,761
It's not what you believe,
it's what you can prove.

589
00:33:33,845 --> 00:33:36,347
And I-- I recognize
that you have to somehow start out

590
00:33:36,431 --> 00:33:39,308
with a worldview,
and you have to investigate it.

591
00:33:39,809 --> 00:33:42,145
<i>Your job is to search out the facts</i>

592
00:33:42,645 --> 00:33:44,689
<i>and to be able to prove what you write.</i>

593
00:33:45,440 --> 00:33:47,692
<i>Is it true? Is it verifiable?</i>

594
00:33:48,401 --> 00:33:49,777
Because it can be libel.

595
00:33:49,861 --> 00:33:51,070
-Well--
-You can hurt people.

596
00:33:51,154 --> 00:33:53,781
{\an8}We've asked the questions
and we've done the research,

597
00:33:53,865 --> 00:33:56,325
{\an8}<i>and still nobody wants to listen to us.</i>

598
00:33:56,826 --> 00:33:59,704
{\an8}We dropped the tape off
at the <i>Times Herald-Record.</i>

599
00:33:59,787 --> 00:34:01,581
{\an8}-And nobody saw it.
-[Gary] Mm-hmm.

600
00:34:01,664 --> 00:34:04,042
We've done the research, but nobody's--

601
00:34:04,125 --> 00:34:06,544
It's tough.
Sometimes people don't read the paper too.

602
00:34:06,627 --> 00:34:08,254
I recognize it. It's tough.

603
00:34:08,337 --> 00:34:10,631
[girl] We have an extra tape
if you'd like to have one now.

604
00:34:10,715 --> 00:34:12,175
I don't really want to see your tape.

605
00:34:12,258 --> 00:34:14,010
<i>If your tape was dropped off at the paper,</i>

606
00:34:14,093 --> 00:34:16,095
<i>I'm sure somebody at the papers</i>
<i>looked at it.</i>

607
00:34:17,972 --> 00:34:20,683
<i>What you've discovered there</i>
<i>may not be news.</i>

608
00:34:20,767 --> 00:34:23,561
<i>It may be something</i>
<i>that everybody's working on.</i>

609
00:34:23,644 --> 00:34:26,814
<i>It may be something that people</i>
<i>are working very hard to solve.</i>

610
00:34:27,440 --> 00:34:28,941
<i>My question to you, I guess, is,</i>

611
00:34:29,025 --> 00:34:32,278
<i>what is it you think you have</i>
<i>that hasn't been reported,</i>

612
00:34:33,404 --> 00:34:35,031
<i>and how do you know it's true?</i>

613
00:34:35,114 --> 00:34:36,699
[tense music plays]

614
00:34:40,328 --> 00:34:43,998
I'm n-nervous for that kid, you know?

615
00:34:45,249 --> 00:34:49,545
I think I started doubting
if any of-- of what I was saying was true.

616
00:34:50,129 --> 00:34:52,757
I'd like to thank Mr. Grossman again
for stopping by,

617
00:34:52,840 --> 00:34:56,094
and thank you all
for participating the way you did.

618
00:34:56,177 --> 00:34:58,096
-[students applaud]
-Thank you.

619
00:34:59,847 --> 00:35:02,934
{\an8}[school bell rings]

620
00:35:03,976 --> 00:35:05,728
[Fred] Okay. [clears throat]

621
00:35:11,567 --> 00:35:13,361
I'd like to introduce you to…

622
00:35:15,404 --> 00:35:17,824
my wife, Denise.

623
00:35:18,324 --> 00:35:21,577
My daughter… Sadie,

624
00:35:21,661 --> 00:35:23,663
and our snowman.

625
00:35:25,748 --> 00:35:27,625
And me. I'm…

626
00:35:29,377 --> 00:35:31,170
I'm, uh, Daddy.

627
00:35:33,297 --> 00:35:34,924
I think I was generally pretty good

628
00:35:35,007 --> 00:35:38,094
about separating
school time from family time.

629
00:35:38,928 --> 00:35:40,429
But still, there were days

630
00:35:40,513 --> 00:35:44,433
when thinking about our investigation
was pretty consuming.

631
00:35:44,517 --> 00:35:46,310
Come on, let's raise some hell.

632
00:35:46,394 --> 00:35:47,436
Here.

633
00:35:48,896 --> 00:35:50,314
[rattles]

634
00:35:51,315 --> 00:35:53,359
[students chatter indistinctly]

635
00:35:53,442 --> 00:35:56,112
[past Fred] Yeah, so just go
off in that direction, looking serious.

636
00:35:56,195 --> 00:35:58,030
So everybody, quiet on the set.
Here we go.

637
00:35:58,114 --> 00:36:00,116
-[boy 1] Throw a snowball.
-[boy 2] Okay.

638
00:36:00,700 --> 00:36:01,701
My name is Jeff Dutemple,

639
00:36:01,784 --> 00:36:03,786
and I'm a student
in Middletown High School.

640
00:36:03,870 --> 00:36:06,664
[Fred] Okay, wait, stop. We're gonna take
that part out, we decided.

641
00:36:06,747 --> 00:36:09,750
-[Jeff] Okay. Okay.
-Just a little bit louder, but not much.

642
00:36:10,501 --> 00:36:11,544
[Jeff] This old dump is s--

643
00:36:11,627 --> 00:36:13,671
-Is a sym-- Ooh.
-[Fred] Start with "this old dump."

644
00:36:13,754 --> 00:36:16,549
Yeah. This old dump is symbolic
of the general failure

645
00:36:16,632 --> 00:36:18,718
of our government to protect us.

646
00:36:19,385 --> 00:36:21,762
[Fred] Okay, hang on.
That was good, except this thing.

647
00:36:21,846 --> 00:36:23,848
I'm gonna have to go on manual.

648
00:36:23,931 --> 00:36:25,766
[Jeff] During my freshman year
of high school,

649
00:36:25,850 --> 00:36:30,438
my dad got into some criminal issues.

650
00:36:30,521 --> 00:36:35,359
He-- He got busted
for basically stealing from his company.

651
00:36:36,152 --> 00:36:38,279
I don't even know if they do this anymore,

652
00:36:38,362 --> 00:36:40,323
uh, but he served weekends.

653
00:36:40,406 --> 00:36:42,366
He was-- He served weekend jail.

654
00:36:42,450 --> 00:36:46,037
So we would drop him off
at like 5:00 p.m. on Friday

655
00:36:46,120 --> 00:36:48,831
and then pick him up,
uh, 5:00 p.m. on Sunday.

656
00:36:48,915 --> 00:36:51,292
[serene ethereal music plays]

657
00:36:51,375 --> 00:36:52,960
He was an alcoholic.

658
00:36:53,461 --> 00:36:57,256
You know, I think he always felt bad
about how his life turned out.

659
00:37:00,134 --> 00:37:03,429
[Fred] Okay, if I can just get a shot
of you standing here looking serious,

660
00:37:03,512 --> 00:37:04,847
like right there.

661
00:37:04,931 --> 00:37:06,140
Come a little bit closer.

662
00:37:06,224 --> 00:37:08,142
[Jeff] Wait. Get a close-up of me?

663
00:37:08,226 --> 00:37:09,477
[Fred] Try not to laugh.

664
00:37:09,560 --> 00:37:13,397
[Jeff] I 100% think that, um, Fred,

665
00:37:13,481 --> 00:37:15,942
you know, he didn't know it,

666
00:37:16,025 --> 00:37:19,737
and, you know, I probably didn't
recognize it at the time,

667
00:37:19,820 --> 00:37:23,991
but looking back on it,
he was a good role model for me.

668
00:37:25,243 --> 00:37:28,371
[Fred] Okay, now reverse it.

669
00:37:28,454 --> 00:37:30,957
Walk toward us and stop.

670
00:37:31,040 --> 00:37:32,792
[Jeff] And there was no judgment.

671
00:37:35,336 --> 00:37:36,587
He's sneaky. [chuckles]

672
00:37:36,671 --> 00:37:40,091
Like, he never-- He never felt
like a teacher, you know?

673
00:37:40,174 --> 00:37:42,593
And-- And, like, in the best way possible.

674
00:37:42,677 --> 00:37:48,391
Like, he talked to every student

675
00:37:49,100 --> 00:37:51,686
as if they were an equal.

676
00:37:51,769 --> 00:37:53,896
The-- [laughs] Cut!

677
00:37:53,980 --> 00:37:56,857
[student] Jeff, did I say a word?
Did I even make any facial expressions?

678
00:37:56,941 --> 00:38:00,361
[Fred] My lens is getting all wet.
We got it. We're good.

679
00:38:06,158 --> 00:38:10,246
We would work after school,
on weekends, during vacations.

680
00:38:10,329 --> 00:38:13,082
Garbage never sleeps,
so we were… [chuckles]

681
00:38:13,165 --> 00:38:14,500
We would work anytime.

682
00:38:14,583 --> 00:38:16,585
[gentle suspenseful music plays]

683
00:38:31,934 --> 00:38:34,061
[Fred reading]

684
00:38:41,736 --> 00:38:43,487
For a lot of families in Middletown,

685
00:38:43,571 --> 00:38:45,573
it's a lot of just stay under the radar.

686
00:38:45,656 --> 00:38:47,533
Right? Like, don't get in trouble.

687
00:38:47,616 --> 00:38:50,202
Don't be too vocal. Don't be too visible.

688
00:38:50,286 --> 00:38:52,705
A lot of people had
a lot of negative things to say about us.

689
00:38:52,788 --> 00:38:55,541
A lot of people judged us,
a lot of people lab-- labeled us

690
00:38:55,624 --> 00:38:58,502
