00:50:15,595 --> 00:50:18,682
[Fred] We realized
that we could make a movie

916
00:50:18,765 --> 00:50:21,518
that would make all of this come to life.

917
00:50:21,601 --> 00:50:25,522
Conservative old-school teachers
thought it was a joke, pretty much.

918
00:50:25,605 --> 00:50:27,232
You know, "How you gonna do this?"

919
00:50:27,315 --> 00:50:32,029
Making a movie is not
like writing a five-page essay.

920
00:50:32,112 --> 00:50:34,823
And, uh, I'm thinking to myself,
"Yeah, it's harder."

921
00:50:35,699 --> 00:50:37,659
[Rachel] "We're making
a documentary film."

922
00:50:37,743 --> 00:50:40,620
I remember using those words
with my parents.

923
00:50:40,704 --> 00:50:43,582
"Documentary" to them
felt like PBS, boring,

924
00:50:43,665 --> 00:50:44,958
so that felt safe.

925
00:50:45,042 --> 00:50:46,543
Right? If I had said, "I'm an activist,

926
00:50:46,626 --> 00:50:49,212
and I'm, like, taking down
corrupt politicians,"

927
00:50:49,296 --> 00:50:51,006
they would have been like, "No."

928
00:50:51,089 --> 00:50:52,424
[past Rachel speaks on video]

929
00:50:52,507 --> 00:50:54,342
And I said,
"Let's show it to the community

930
00:50:54,426 --> 00:50:56,344
and things are going to start to fire up."

931
00:50:56,428 --> 00:50:58,055
[people chatter]

932
00:50:58,138 --> 00:51:00,432
This is Dean Dyche for MHS TV News.

933
00:51:00,515 --> 00:51:04,144
Here we have Angela Townsend, and who--
who has just wrote a poem on toxic waste.

934
00:51:04,728 --> 00:51:09,107
<i>He smiles a happy, tinkling smile</i>
<i>As he paints Mom's fingers red</i>

935
00:51:09,191 --> 00:51:11,318
<i>And if he spills it in the pond</i>

936
00:51:11,401 --> 00:51:13,361
<i>Three minnows may soon be dead</i>

937
00:51:14,196 --> 00:51:17,282
As you can see at this meeting tonight,
we've had a very big turnout.

938
00:51:17,783 --> 00:51:19,951
We have all local politicians
from Bloomingburg.

939
00:51:20,035 --> 00:51:22,996
We have Cable 6, <i>Times Herald-Record.</i>

940
00:51:25,916 --> 00:51:27,709
[opening film music plays]

941
00:51:30,837 --> 00:51:33,048
{\an8}[Jeff] <i>These photos</i>
<i>of the old town of Wallkill Dump</i>

942
00:51:33,131 --> 00:51:34,299
{\an8}<i>were taken by an agent</i>

943
00:51:34,382 --> 00:51:38,178
{\an8}<i>of the New York State Department</i>
<i>of Environmental Conservation in 1976,</i>

944
00:51:38,261 --> 00:51:40,680
{\an8}<i>the year most of my classmates</i>
<i>and I were born.</i>

945
00:51:40,764 --> 00:51:43,725
<i>Our plan was to put together</i>
<i>a video documentary</i>

946
00:51:43,809 --> 00:51:46,770
<i>to inform the public,</i>
<i>to move politicians into action,</i>

947
00:51:46,853 --> 00:51:49,898
<i>and to attract some media attention</i>
<i>to open up the story.</i>

948
00:51:49,981 --> 00:51:52,275
We hoped that the problems
would be taken seriously

949
00:51:52,359 --> 00:51:54,486
and a full investigation
would have been conducted

950
00:51:54,569 --> 00:51:56,738
into what happened here, but it hasn't,

951
00:51:56,822 --> 00:51:59,908
even though we've tried our hardest
to get the tape into the right hands.

952
00:52:00,742 --> 00:52:02,410
[Wade] <i>This is my first time at this site.</i>

953
00:52:02,494 --> 00:52:06,206
Obviously, this is one
huge pyramid of pollution.

954
00:52:06,706 --> 00:52:09,042
And I don't think there is a soul on earth

955
00:52:09,126 --> 00:52:12,921
that could tell you all the toxics
that are in here or their distribution.

956
00:52:13,505 --> 00:52:16,133
[man] <i>Dealing with substances</i>
<i>that cannot be removed.</i>

957
00:52:16,216 --> 00:52:18,426
<i>It might take 50 years of treatment,</i>

958
00:52:18,510 --> 00:52:21,847
{\an8}pumping and flushing
and recycling of water

959
00:52:21,930 --> 00:52:23,932
{\an8}before you can clean out
some of these aquifers.

960
00:52:24,015 --> 00:52:25,892
{\an8}Fifty, seventy-five, a hundred years?

961
00:52:25,976 --> 00:52:28,770
[Jeff] <i>The Orange County Health Department</i>
<i>has never done a survey</i>

962
00:52:28,854 --> 00:52:31,731
<i>to determine the effects</i>
<i>of water pollution on public health.</i>

963
00:52:31,815 --> 00:52:35,652
<i>Does it have to be this way?</i>
<i>Who will speak up for the people?</i>

964
00:52:35,735 --> 00:52:37,320
[audience applauds]

965
00:52:39,781 --> 00:52:42,868
-[woman 1] Is the mayor here tonight?
-[man] He was. I don't know where he is.

966
00:52:42,951 --> 00:52:45,871
[woman 2] This is what I scoop
out from my cellar.

967
00:52:45,954 --> 00:52:48,248
[teenager] Send it to the mayor
for drinking water.

968
00:52:48,331 --> 00:52:49,499
[people laugh]

969
00:52:50,792 --> 00:52:51,918
[man] I'm here tonight

970
00:52:52,002 --> 00:52:56,047
{\an8}just letting you know that-- that people
are deeply concerned about this

971
00:52:56,131 --> 00:52:58,216
{\an8}within the government
of the state of New York,

972
00:52:58,300 --> 00:53:00,093
{\an8}and that, uh, we will launch

973
00:53:00,177 --> 00:53:03,889
an investigation into this situation

974
00:53:03,972 --> 00:53:06,892
and protect the resources,
the, uh-- the water supplies

975
00:53:06,975 --> 00:53:09,477
to all, of course, the residents
of, uh-- of the community.

976
00:53:09,561 --> 00:53:11,563
[audience applauds]

977
00:53:12,522 --> 00:53:14,524
[tense ethereal music plays]

978
00:53:15,192 --> 00:53:18,695
[Fred] It was a huge deal
that Assemblyman Hinchey was there.

979
00:53:19,237 --> 00:53:21,615
<i>We have here in the Hudson River Valley,</i>

980
00:53:21,698 --> 00:53:25,744
<i>one of the most important,</i>
<i>significant, historical,</i>

981
00:53:25,827 --> 00:53:28,705
<i>and environmental resources</i>
<i>that this state possesses.</i>

982
00:53:29,456 --> 00:53:32,334
[Fred] When he was a rookie,
he was sent to Love Canal

983
00:53:32,417 --> 00:53:35,587
to observe and to investigate

984
00:53:35,670 --> 00:53:38,131
and see if the whole story was being told.

985
00:53:38,215 --> 00:53:40,175
He had a name, he had a reputation.

986
00:53:40,258 --> 00:53:43,470
He was a maverick, a crusader.
He was all those things.

987
00:53:43,553 --> 00:53:44,554
So I'm thinking,

988
00:53:44,638 --> 00:53:47,974
"We're gonna get these guys,
we're gonna clean things up."

989
00:53:48,058 --> 00:53:49,684
"We're rolling. We got 'em."

990
00:53:51,061 --> 00:53:52,896
{\an8}[girl] <i>Good evening,</i>
<i>and welcome to MHS TV.</i>

991
00:53:52,979 --> 00:53:55,899
{\an8}<i>Tonight, we have more general high school</i>
<i>section nine soccer highlights,</i>

992
00:53:55,982 --> 00:53:57,817
{\an8}<i>a book week presentation, and more.</i>

993
00:53:57,901 --> 00:53:59,527
[ominous music plays]

994
00:53:59,611 --> 00:54:02,948
-[peppy music plays]
<i>-♪ Nuclear war, there's no reason for ♪</i>

995
00:54:03,031 --> 00:54:05,033
[spacey music plays]

996
00:54:07,535 --> 00:54:10,080
-Hi, I'm Jeff Dutemple.
-And I'm Rachel Raimist.

997
00:54:10,163 --> 00:54:12,249
On Friday, March 20th, 1992,

998
00:54:12,332 --> 00:54:14,918
Maurice Hinchey,
assemblyman from Saugerties

999
00:54:15,001 --> 00:54:17,170
and the head of the task force
on the environment,

1000
00:54:17,254 --> 00:54:20,048
held a hearing
regarding the Wallkill Landfill.

1001
00:54:20,131 --> 00:54:23,718
Hinchey questioned many witnesses,
including former employees at the site,

1002
00:54:23,802 --> 00:54:26,221
local residents,
and Ward Stone, the toxic avenger.

1003
00:54:26,304 --> 00:54:27,806
Here are excerpts from the meeting.

1004
00:54:27,889 --> 00:54:30,767
[Jeff] Oh, my God,
we filmed so many hearings.

1005
00:54:31,351 --> 00:54:33,812
I think if we went to a meeting,

1006
00:54:34,437 --> 00:54:36,189
it counted as a grade.

1007
00:54:36,273 --> 00:54:40,151
Like the core group who was working on it
at that time, we just liked going.

1008
00:54:40,235 --> 00:54:41,695
So the grade didn't matter.

1009
00:54:41,778 --> 00:54:45,115
Do you have any way of estimating
the number of paper drums

1010
00:54:45,198 --> 00:54:47,033
that may have been deposited there?

1011
00:54:47,117 --> 00:54:49,035
I daresay at least a thousand.

1012
00:54:49,744 --> 00:54:53,540
No, I looked down, and this bag
was sticking up through the soil.

1013
00:54:54,291 --> 00:54:57,919
And there was one word
that I could see, and it was "lead."

1014
00:54:59,087 --> 00:55:01,840
It's a product called Lactra-60.

1015
00:55:02,716 --> 00:55:05,677
And it's a stabilizer used in plastics.

1016
00:55:07,053 --> 00:55:09,723
This kind of powder, could it--
could it do permanent damage?

1017
00:55:09,806 --> 00:55:11,224
It could kill you.

1018
00:55:12,475 --> 00:55:15,812
[reporter] Some students put together
a video, which I'm sure you're aware of.

1019
00:55:15,895 --> 00:55:17,689
And the conclusions
that they draw from this

1020
00:55:17,772 --> 00:55:19,691
is that it was a political cover-up here.

1021
00:55:19,774 --> 00:55:21,901
Do you think there's validity
to that comment?

1022
00:55:21,985 --> 00:55:23,486
I think that there is validity to that.

1023
00:55:23,570 --> 00:55:24,571
[man] I understand.

1024
00:55:24,654 --> 00:55:26,865
[Hinchey] The town has,
in all probability, participated

1025
00:55:26,948 --> 00:55:30,285
at some level in dumping
that occurred at this particular site.

1026
00:55:30,368 --> 00:55:33,330
{\an8}I think that, to some extent,
there has been a cover-up here.

1027
00:55:34,497 --> 00:55:38,168
[Jeff] It was a moment
of legitimacy for us.

1028
00:55:38,251 --> 00:55:39,461
[Hinchey] Thanks for your help.

1029
00:55:39,544 --> 00:55:41,087
-We'll see you.
-Yep.

1030
00:55:41,171 --> 00:55:43,173
-Take it easy.
-Bye.

1031
00:55:43,256 --> 00:55:47,635
I mean, a lot of these things that were
once dismissed as conspiracies are…

1032
00:55:47,719 --> 00:55:49,262
There's some basis to all of them.

1033
00:55:49,346 --> 00:55:51,097
-[Hinchey] Well…
-[man 2] All right.

1034
00:55:51,181 --> 00:55:52,349
{\an8}Hopefully, we'll resolve it.

1035
00:55:52,432 --> 00:55:54,267
{\an8}Hopefully you'll resolve it.
I hope so, yes.

1036
00:55:54,351 --> 00:55:57,354
{\an8}I-- I think we're-- we're on our way
to resolving it.

1037
00:55:57,937 --> 00:56:00,273
We will, uh, look forward
to the additional results

1038
00:56:00,357 --> 00:56:01,983
of tests that were taken by Mr. Stone.

1039
00:56:02,067 --> 00:56:05,320
And I think that they could be
possibly very revealing.

1040
00:56:05,820 --> 00:56:07,530
That-- That ends this hearing.

1041
00:56:08,073 --> 00:56:11,242
[people applaud]

1042
00:56:13,787 --> 00:56:15,080
[easy listening music plays]

1043
00:56:20,001 --> 00:56:22,003
[suspenseful music plays]

1044
00:56:23,630 --> 00:56:25,757
-[phone rings]
-[Fred] <i>April 7th.</i>

1045
00:56:25,840 --> 00:56:27,258
[Fred reading]

1046
00:56:29,177 --> 00:56:31,930
<i>He says he went back to the dump</i>
<i>to do more research,</i>

1047
00:56:32,013 --> 00:56:35,058
<i>but the town supervisor</i>
<i>had him thrown off the site.</i>

1048
00:56:36,476 --> 00:56:39,521
<i>They even confiscated his samples</i>
<i>when he tried to leave.</i>

1049
00:56:40,105 --> 00:56:42,649
<i>They've decided to conduct</i>
<i>their own investigation</i>

1050
00:56:42,732 --> 00:56:44,150
<i>and Ward's not part of it.</i>

1051
00:56:45,360 --> 00:56:48,405
{\an8}<i>So we decided we'd all go together.</i>

1052
00:56:48,988 --> 00:56:51,408
[Rachel] We made a decision
to tip off the local media

1053
00:56:51,491 --> 00:56:52,617
that we'd be there.

1054
00:56:53,660 --> 00:56:59,124
We followed Ward filming,
and then the news filmed us filming Ward.

1055
00:57:00,083 --> 00:57:03,128
There was a bit of spectacle
involved with that.

1056
00:57:04,045 --> 00:57:06,381
-What have you got to hide?
-I have nothing to hide, sir.

1057
00:57:06,464 --> 00:57:08,216
-Yes, you do.
-There are no-- Mr. Stone.

1058
00:57:08,299 --> 00:57:09,968
-The agreement--
-You got something to hide.

1059
00:57:10,051 --> 00:57:13,179
The agreement with Assemblyman Hinchey
and your offices--

1060
00:57:13,263 --> 00:57:14,139
Not my office.

1061
00:57:14,222 --> 00:57:18,560
You can do anything you'd like here, sir,
as long as my engineers are present.

1062
00:57:18,643 --> 00:57:20,019
Get 'em. Get 'em, I'm here.

1063
00:57:20,103 --> 00:57:21,312
You want to come here and work,

1064
00:57:21,396 --> 00:57:23,398
you'll come here and work
when my engineers are here.

1065
00:57:23,481 --> 00:57:25,567
I cover the entire state of New York!

1066
00:57:25,650 --> 00:57:28,111
I came. I don't work on bankers' hours.

1067
00:57:28,194 --> 00:57:31,906
-Were there engineers with you?
-I was here in the time span.

1068
00:57:31,990 --> 00:57:34,200
-Sir, were the engineers--
-I was here in the-- Sir!

1069
00:57:37,036 --> 00:57:39,998
[Rachel] I always thought
politicians were just humoring us

1070
00:57:40,081 --> 00:57:44,210
because it was like cute and, you know,
civic engagement with young people.

1071
00:57:44,294 --> 00:57:47,839
But that day with Ward, that wasn't cute.

1072
00:57:47,922 --> 00:57:52,677
We were definitely meddling kids
in the middle of things.

1073
00:57:52,760 --> 00:57:55,221
And it was-- It felt heated.
The police came.

1074
00:57:55,305 --> 00:57:56,848
Do you understand what I'm telling you?

1075
00:57:56,931 --> 00:57:58,850
I understand that you have
something to cover up.

1076
00:57:58,933 --> 00:58:02,061
You don't want to see data
that is in contrast to you.

1077
00:58:02,145 --> 00:58:03,605
-Understand what I'm telling you?
-No.

1078
00:58:03,688 --> 00:58:05,815
-You don't understand?
-I understand what you're saying.

1079
00:58:05,899 --> 00:58:06,858
I am not accepting it

1080
00:58:06,941 --> 00:58:09,694
till I hear something different
from our law enforcement.

1081
00:58:09,777 --> 00:58:11,488
We're closed.
You're not taking any samples.

1082
00:58:11,571 --> 00:58:13,114
We'll see. We'll see.

1083
00:58:13,198 --> 00:58:15,408
Get a court order, and I'll honor it.

1084
00:58:15,492 --> 00:58:17,452
I want to look around
if I don't take samples.

1085
00:58:17,535 --> 00:58:19,496
No, sir. You're not doing anything.
You're leaving.

1086
00:58:19,579 --> 00:58:20,872
You're a problem.

1087
00:58:22,081 --> 00:58:23,917
Mr. Stone, this is private property.

1088
00:58:24,000 --> 00:58:25,543
-[Ward] Public property.
-No, sir.

1089
00:58:25,627 --> 00:58:27,837
You have been advised to leave
the property at this time.

1090
00:58:27,921 --> 00:58:29,631
Do you know of any violation of the law?

1091
00:58:29,714 --> 00:58:32,175
[cop] Violation of the law is
right now you are trespassing.

1092
00:58:32,258 --> 00:58:34,302
You've been asked to leave several times.

1093
00:58:34,385 --> 00:58:36,262
You do not leave,
I'm gonna have to arrest you.

1094
00:58:39,432 --> 00:58:40,975
[Rachel] Jeff, stop doing that.

1095
00:58:41,559 --> 00:58:43,561
[dialogue inaudible]

1096
00:58:49,234 --> 00:58:51,194
[telephone rings]

1097
00:58:51,277 --> 00:58:53,696
[Fred reading]

1098
00:58:54,614 --> 00:58:56,241
[mysterious music plays]

1099
00:58:57,033 --> 00:58:59,661
<i>He is an intense man</i>
<i>with plenty of swearing,</i>

1100
00:58:59,744 --> 00:59:03,665
<i>and pauses for Jesus to strike him dead</i>
<i>if he is not telling the truth.</i>

1101
00:59:08,753 --> 00:59:11,089
<i>He says he worked</i>
<i>with the Orange County Sheriff's Office</i>

1102
00:59:11,172 --> 00:59:13,883
<i>since the early '80s,</i>
<i>and was in charge of the landfill</i>

1103
00:59:13,967 --> 00:59:16,803
<i>until he was removed</i>
<i>for being overzealous.</i>

1104
00:59:18,555 --> 00:59:22,141
<i>Mr. B also claims to have had</i>
<i>toxic samples destroyed</i>

1105
00:59:22,225 --> 00:59:23,851
<i>and notes confiscated.</i>

1106
00:59:24,936 --> 00:59:26,396
<i>"They're all corrupt," says Mr. B.</i>

1107
00:59:31,359 --> 00:59:32,277
<i>I'm starting to wonder</i>

1108
00:59:32,360 --> 00:59:34,362
<i>if all of these interesting</i>
<i>novelistic things</i>

1109
00:59:34,445 --> 00:59:36,197
<i>are really happening to me.</i>

1110
00:59:38,157 --> 00:59:43,121
<i>Or if I am a paranoid conspiracy theorist</i>
<i>who sees plots which aren't really there.</i>

1111
00:59:44,205 --> 00:59:46,416
[students chatter]

1112
00:59:46,499 --> 00:59:47,834
-[videotape rewinding]
-Near this?

1113
00:59:47,917 --> 00:59:49,377
Nah, keep going.

1114
00:59:49,460 --> 00:59:50,962
[Fred] <i>Mr. B wants to meet soon.</i>

1115
00:59:59,470 --> 01:00:02,307
{\an8}[student 1] Handing out fliers,
putting them on cars and things like that.

1116
01:00:02,390 --> 01:00:03,349
{\an8}[student 2 groans]

1117
01:00:03,433 --> 01:00:06,352
{\an8}[student 1] Having a public meeting,
informing people of the county's…

1118
01:00:08,896 --> 01:00:10,356
{\an8}Hi, my name is Trey Long

1119
01:00:10,440 --> 01:00:12,233
with MH-- MHS TV,

1120
01:00:12,317 --> 01:00:16,029
and I'd like to welcome
Mr. Bill Cummings to the set today.

1121
01:00:16,112 --> 01:00:17,822
He's gonna answer some questions for us.

1122
01:00:17,905 --> 01:00:20,325
{\an8}Well, this is probably
where I'm gonna get beat up,

1123
01:00:20,408 --> 01:00:22,619
{\an8}so might as well take it head-on.

1124
01:00:22,702 --> 01:00:24,954
{\an8}Uh, Mr. Stone is a wildlife pathologist,

1125
01:00:25,038 --> 01:00:27,999
and he's supposedly
one of the best in the country.

1126
01:00:28,082 --> 01:00:32,086
{\an8}Um, I've heard some great things
about Mr. Stone.

1127
01:00:32,587 --> 01:00:36,716
{\an8}However, Mr. Stone has
yet to provide anyone

1128
01:00:36,799 --> 01:00:38,134
{\an8}with his test results.

1129
01:00:38,217 --> 01:00:42,764
{\an8}He's made certain accusations that I would
like to see investigated and handled,

1130
01:00:42,847 --> 01:00:44,599
{\an8}um, but you can't investigate

1131
01:00:44,682 --> 01:00:46,851
{\an8}if you don't have the information
in front of you.

1132
01:00:46,934 --> 01:00:48,603
{\an8}[Trey] Mr. Cummings,
you took his samples--

1133
01:00:48,686 --> 01:00:50,021
{\an8}Let me finish, okay?

1134
01:00:50,104 --> 01:00:52,732
{\an8}I'll finish, and you guys
can attack me, okay?

1135
01:00:52,815 --> 01:00:54,901
Because Ward has told-- has told us

1136
01:00:54,984 --> 01:00:56,653
that he thinks
you're covering something up

1137
01:00:56,736 --> 01:00:59,822
by the way you are so adamant
about not letting him see his samples.

1138
01:00:59,906 --> 01:01:02,742
I have to defend the town.
I'm not gonna play pointing fingers.

1139
01:01:02,825 --> 01:01:06,037
He's told us that he's never had
a problem with other towns.

1140
01:01:06,120 --> 01:01:07,872
[Bill] Listen,
he's probably absolutely right.

1141
01:01:07,955 --> 01:01:09,791
As far as not being able
to see the samples,

1142
01:01:09,874 --> 01:01:11,626
even though the town engineers
weren't there.

1143
01:01:11,709 --> 01:01:13,544
He's absolutely right.
Uh, what I felt was,

1144
01:01:13,628 --> 01:01:16,214
this has become
such a critical, sensitive issue

1145
01:01:16,297 --> 01:01:18,841
that everybody
is constantly pointing fingers,

1146
01:01:18,925 --> 01:01:21,552
and talking about corruption,
talking about this, talking about that,

1147
01:01:21,636 --> 01:01:23,012
that we have to work together.

1148
01:01:23,096 --> 01:01:27,350
Um, I'm just gonna chan-- get away
from the landfill for a second.

1149
01:01:27,433 --> 01:01:31,396
This is a map of the town
of Wallkill and, um, Middletown,

1150
01:01:31,896 --> 01:01:35,191
and its plan for development.

1151
01:01:35,274 --> 01:01:37,985
And it's color-coded.
Trey did a very nice job here.

1152
01:01:38,069 --> 01:01:39,195
Thanks, Jeff.

1153
01:01:40,405 --> 01:01:43,324
We've been told, and as we understand,

1154
01:01:43,408 --> 01:01:47,412
this-- most of this land that's zoned
for industrial and commercial use

1155
01:01:47,495 --> 01:01:50,039
is, um, owned by past politicians

1156
01:01:50,123 --> 01:01:53,835
of either the town of Wallkill
or Orange County.

1157
01:01:54,460 --> 01:01:57,755
[Bill] Uh, yeah, there's no question
that certain families

1158
01:01:57,839 --> 01:02:02,510
have a lot of, um, land interest
within the town of Wallkill, absolutely.

1159
01:02:02,593 --> 01:02:03,803
[Jeff] Okay.

1160
01:02:03,886 --> 01:02:06,055
[Trey] The reason we're asking
is because we've seen

1161
01:02:06,139 --> 01:02:07,598
from a lot of different people

1162
01:02:07,682 --> 01:02:10,685
saying that the politicians
have basically bought land,

1163
01:02:10,768 --> 01:02:12,353
got involved in politics,

1164
01:02:12,437 --> 01:02:16,107
zoned their own land
for limited skyscraper apartments

1165
01:02:16,190 --> 01:02:17,525
or commercial districts,

1166
01:02:17,608 --> 01:02:19,861
and then planned
to sell it off in the future.

1167
01:02:19,944 --> 01:02:21,946
{\an8}[tense chiming music plays]

1168
01:02:23,656 --> 01:02:26,701
[Mike] I think a lot of it came
down to parcels of land

1169
01:02:26,784 --> 01:02:29,162
that developers had their eye on.

1170
01:02:29,245 --> 01:02:32,790
If news gets out
that some of these properties are tainted,

1171
01:02:32,874 --> 01:02:34,041
they become worthless.

1172
01:02:34,125 --> 01:02:36,127
{\an8}[townspeople chatter indistinctly]

1173
01:02:41,007 --> 01:02:44,594
When we arrived,
I remember one of the city councilmen

1174
01:02:44,677 --> 01:02:47,096
saying to me, "What are you doing here?"

1175
01:02:47,180 --> 01:02:49,891
I said, "I'm filming the board meeting."
And he said, "Why?"

1176
01:02:49,974 --> 01:02:51,017
Kind of with a sneer.

1177
01:02:51,100 --> 01:02:53,603
I said, "I'm here-- We're filming
the meeting like a journalist."

1178
01:02:53,686 --> 01:02:55,605
And he said, "You're not a journalist."

1179
01:02:55,688 --> 01:02:57,732
First off, for those of you
who are not from the town,

1180
01:02:57,815 --> 01:03:00,485
my name is Bill Cummings,
I am the town supervisor.

1181
01:03:00,568 --> 01:03:03,654
This public hearing
is on the topic of the landfill,

1182
01:03:04,322 --> 01:03:08,826
and the different alternatives
with what we should do with the landfill.

1183
01:03:09,786 --> 01:03:12,246
The basic findings
were minor contamination

1184
01:03:12,330 --> 01:03:16,834
by various organic chemicals and metals
on the site only.

1185
01:03:16,918 --> 01:03:20,004
As a result, there is
no imminent or immediate threat

1186
01:03:20,087 --> 01:03:22,089
to human health or the environment.

1187
01:03:22,673 --> 01:03:26,385
The landfill is a source
of minor contamination,

1188
01:03:26,469 --> 01:03:29,180
but does not pose a long-term
unacceptable risk

1189
01:03:29,263 --> 01:03:32,558
to either human health or the environment.

1190
01:03:34,560 --> 01:03:37,480
The alternative
that's been selected by the town

1191
01:03:37,563 --> 01:03:39,607
and by the state is alternative three.

1192
01:03:40,191 --> 01:03:43,319
Construction of a cap
over the entire landfill.

1193
01:03:43,402 --> 01:03:45,404
[ominous music plays]

1194
01:03:49,951 --> 01:03:52,537
I remember thinking, "Like a pool cover?"

1195
01:03:52,620 --> 01:03:56,040
Like, they're just gonna cover it
so we don't look at it?

1196
01:03:56,123 --> 01:03:57,667
Sounds like a very dumb idea

1197
01:03:57,750 --> 01:04:01,754
because you cover something,
but it's still dripping into the earth.

1198
01:04:01,838 --> 01:04:06,217
It-- It felt to me like an insult
on top of an injury.

1199
01:04:06,300 --> 01:04:10,930
I understand that water can be
contaminated miles away

1200
01:04:11,013 --> 01:04:13,808
and follow a stream under the ground.

1201
01:04:13,891 --> 01:04:16,352
So I think that you definitely
should follow through

1202
01:04:16,435 --> 01:04:19,397
and make sure
because I have grandchildren,

1203
01:04:19,480 --> 01:04:20,982
I work at the school system,

1204
01:04:21,065 --> 01:04:25,194
I-- I have many friends in the area, and…

1205
01:04:25,278 --> 01:04:26,904
it's on your conscience.

1206
01:04:26,988 --> 01:04:27,947
Please.

1207
01:04:28,823 --> 01:04:31,701
[woman] Did you do any autopsies
on any of the animals?

1208
01:04:31,784 --> 01:04:33,953
[councillor] No.
There was no, uh, tissue analysis done

1209
01:04:34,036 --> 01:04:34,954
or anything like that.

1210
01:04:35,037 --> 01:04:37,456
-I had-- I had a pony who died.
-Mm-hmm.

1211
01:04:37,540 --> 01:04:39,584
And we did have an autopsy done on her,

1212
01:04:39,667 --> 01:04:41,919
and there was toxic chemicals in her.

1213
01:04:42,420 --> 01:04:45,381
-[councillor] Well, I think--
-And she-- We live a quarter mile away.

1214
01:04:46,883 --> 01:04:49,051
[man] Does anybody else want to speak?

1215
01:04:49,135 --> 01:04:51,429
Just maybe in the back.

1216
01:04:52,555 --> 01:04:54,974
I wasn't raised like,
"Your voice is important!"

1217
01:04:55,057 --> 01:04:56,809
"We want to hear what you have to say."

1218
01:04:56,893 --> 01:05:00,187
It was like, "Be quiet,
do what you're supposed to do." You know?

1219
01:05:00,271 --> 01:05:03,107
I had a lot of trouble
with the "be quiet" part.

1220
01:05:04,483 --> 01:05:06,485
[people chatter on video]

1221
01:05:10,406 --> 01:05:11,949
My name is Rachel Raimist,

1222
01:05:12,033 --> 01:05:14,535
and I'm a student
at Middletown High School.

1223
01:05:14,619 --> 01:05:17,121
And I just feel that I can
no longer stand in the background

1224
01:05:17,204 --> 01:05:18,748
and document this on film.

1225
01:05:18,831 --> 01:05:23,753
Because this-- this story is an example
for me and for my generation.

1226
01:05:23,836 --> 01:05:26,422
And all I ask
is that you really investigate

1227
01:05:26,505 --> 01:05:27,965
the situation to the fullest.

1228
01:05:28,049 --> 01:05:31,302
<i>Because it seems to me</i>
<i>that putting a cap on this landfill</i>

1229
01:05:31,385 --> 01:05:33,471
<i>is merely just putting a cover on it</i>

1230
01:05:33,554 --> 01:05:35,348
<i>and saying, "Okay, we'll just look at it,</i>

1231
01:05:35,431 --> 01:05:37,475
and we'll deal with it in 30 years."

1232
01:05:37,558 --> 01:05:39,018
Well, I'm gonna have to deal with it.

1233
01:05:39,101 --> 01:05:40,853
And your children are gonna
have to deal with it,

1234
01:05:40,937 --> 01:05:43,105
and my children
are gonna have to deal with it.

1235
01:05:43,189 --> 01:05:47,109
And it's really, really difficult
to come here and listen to you say,

1236
01:05:47,193 --> 01:05:48,653
"Okay, we'll just cover this up

1237
01:05:48,736 --> 01:05:51,072
and we'll deal with it later
without fully investigating."

1238
01:05:51,155 --> 01:05:54,033
Because this is a crime against humanity.

1239
01:05:54,116 --> 01:05:56,118
[townspeople applaud]

1240
01:05:59,789 --> 01:06:00,748
[applauding on video]

1241
01:06:00,831 --> 01:06:01,832
Yeah.

1242
01:06:02,333 --> 01:06:03,376
I wasn't wrong.

1243
01:06:03,459 --> 01:06:05,920
[mellow music plays]

1244
01:06:10,299 --> 01:06:14,470
On TV, Middletown was picture-perfect.

1245
01:06:15,388 --> 01:06:18,516
But there's a very high rate of cancer

1246
01:06:18,599 --> 01:06:20,726
in that whole surrounding area.

1247
01:06:23,020 --> 01:06:26,732
My own parents got sick and got cancer

1248
01:06:26,816 --> 01:06:30,528
and passed away almost 30 years ago.

1249
01:06:30,611 --> 01:06:32,530
But there was a time when my mom was sick,

1250
01:06:32,613 --> 01:06:35,116
her friend was sick,
the other lady down the street was sick.

1251
01:06:35,199 --> 01:06:38,661
Like, everyone was, like, waving
on their way to chemo.

1252
01:06:38,744 --> 01:06:43,833
It's such a hard thing to pursue,
except anecdotally.

1253
01:06:43,916 --> 01:06:46,502
To-- To say, "Look,
here's a road near a dump,

1254
01:06:46,585 --> 01:06:49,964
and all these people
who became ill because of it."

1255
01:06:50,047 --> 01:06:53,217
But that's not enough to make the case.

1256
01:06:53,300 --> 01:06:55,219
It's hard for laypeople to do that.

1257
01:06:56,137 --> 01:06:59,140
[past Fred] Okay, tape is rolling,
so any time you're ready.

1258
01:06:59,223 --> 01:07:02,935
Okay. Do you want to do a sound check?

1259
01:07:03,019 --> 01:07:05,354
[past Fred] All right.
Spread it like this.

1260
01:07:05,438 --> 01:07:08,315
[Fred] I mean, Trey Long has cancer now,

1261
01:07:08,983 --> 01:07:12,361
and is asking himself
if growing up in Middletown

1262
01:07:12,445 --> 01:07:13,779
made him sick.

1263
01:07:15,906 --> 01:07:17,491
What-- What can you think about that?

1264
01:07:17,575 --> 01:07:20,286
You know, it's terrible and it's happening
to more and more people.

1265
01:07:22,913 --> 01:07:25,791
[David] I really don't think
it matters where somebody lives.

1266
01:07:27,793 --> 01:07:29,628
Most scientists would tell us

1267
01:07:29,712 --> 01:07:33,424
that, uh, the average American consumes

1268
01:07:33,507 --> 01:07:38,137
the equivalent of a credit card a month
in microplastics, at this point.

1269
01:07:38,220 --> 01:07:41,682
If I shoved a credit card in your mouth,
that would be a crime.

1270
01:07:41,766 --> 01:07:45,436
That would literally be a battery.
I would have assaulted and battered you.

1271
01:07:46,979 --> 01:07:50,524
Yet, when this is done
on a corporate scale,

1272
01:07:50,608 --> 01:07:52,777
everybody just sort of accepts that.

1273
01:07:55,279 --> 01:07:58,824
[Fred] A landfill is
where we dump our secrets.

1274
01:07:59,325 --> 01:08:02,787
Where we put our misdeeds
and then paper them over,

1275
01:08:02,870 --> 01:08:04,038
cover them up.

1276
01:08:07,208 --> 01:08:09,335
We weren't going to let that happen.

1277
01:08:10,503 --> 01:08:13,089
[news anchor] State plans to cap
a toxic landfill in Orange County

1278
01:08:13,172 --> 01:08:14,006
have come under fire.

1279
01:08:14,090 --> 01:08:17,009
Those concerned
about the old town of Wallkill dump

1280
01:08:17,093 --> 01:08:20,054
say they want some answers
before officials seal the site.

1281
01:08:20,137 --> 01:08:23,265
Among those concerned,
a group of high school students.

1282
01:08:24,141 --> 01:08:27,228
{\an8}It's a big environmental issue,
and it's something that…

1283
01:08:27,311 --> 01:08:28,771
{\an8}can really affect our lives.

1284
01:08:28,854 --> 01:08:31,065
{\an8}It can kill people. It's a deadly issue.

1285
01:08:31,148 --> 01:08:33,484
[reporter] <i>Fred Isseks teaches</i>
<i>at Middletown High School.</i>

1286
01:08:33,567 --> 01:08:36,237
<i>He says capping the dump</i>
<i>virtually ignores evidence</i>

1287
01:08:36,320 --> 01:08:38,864
<i>his journalism students</i>
<i>captured on videotape</i>

1288
01:08:38,948 --> 01:08:40,616
<i>in a class project.</i>

1289
01:08:40,699 --> 01:08:44,620
These are kids who, in many instances,
know more about what's going on

1290
01:08:44,703 --> 01:08:46,413
in the county than their parents do.

1291
01:08:47,289 --> 01:08:49,458
[past Jeff] You've got
to get out there and work for it.

1292
01:08:49,542 --> 01:08:52,878
This is what we've been doing. You know,
now we're getting some exposure for it.

1293
01:08:54,588 --> 01:08:56,549
[Jeff] It snowballed, right?

1294
01:08:58,551 --> 01:09:00,970
Once the <i>Village Voice </i>article came out,

1295
01:09:01,053 --> 01:09:02,888
it was significant enough

1296
01:09:02,972 --> 01:09:08,269
that I then became,
like, the Toxic Avenger guy.

1297
01:09:08,352 --> 01:09:10,646
[psychedelic music plays]

1298
01:09:10,729 --> 01:09:14,525
[narrator] <i>Melvin became</i>
<i>the Toxic Avenger,</i>

1299
01:09:14,608 --> 01:09:18,779
<i>the first superhero born</i>
<i>out of nuclear waste.</i>

1300
01:09:21,574 --> 01:09:23,409
[Fred] Yeah, I-- I never saw the movie,

1301
01:09:23,492 --> 01:09:27,413
but more than one news program
used that expression.

1302
01:09:27,997 --> 01:09:29,999
{\an8}[telephone rings]

1303
01:09:30,708 --> 01:09:32,126
[Fred reading]

1304
01:09:39,758 --> 01:09:42,428
<i>There are ten toxic landfills like this</i>
<i>in Orange County,</i>

1305
01:09:42,511 --> 01:09:44,680
<i>all connected to the Mafia.</i>

1306
01:09:45,639 --> 01:09:48,434
<i>"All the drinking water</i>
<i>is polluted," says Mr. B.</i>

1307
01:09:49,226 --> 01:09:51,979
[continues reading]

1308
01:09:56,609 --> 01:10:01,030
[past Fred] He strikes me as a man
who has a real strong sense of justice,

1309
01:10:01,113 --> 01:10:02,031
and he's really angry

1310
01:10:02,114 --> 01:10:04,116
that this kind of thing
has gone on so long

1311
01:10:04,200 --> 01:10:05,451
and nothing's happened.

1312
01:10:05,534 --> 01:10:06,994
So he wants to come in.

1313
01:10:07,536 --> 01:10:08,746
-[boy] In here?
-In here.

1314
01:10:08,829 --> 01:10:10,789
-[girl] With his gun?
-Probably sometime this week.

1315
01:10:10,873 --> 01:10:14,251
No, no, we'll make sure
that he leaves his gun outside.

1316
01:10:16,128 --> 01:10:18,589
{\an8}And five, and ready?

1317
01:10:18,672 --> 01:10:21,383
{\an8}-[boy] Three, two…
-Do this serious, man. Do this serious.

1318
01:10:22,301 --> 01:10:23,636
Welcome to Middletown High School.

1319
01:10:23,719 --> 01:10:26,597
{\an8}Today we're talking with an officer
from Orange County Sheriff's Department

1320
01:10:26,680 --> 01:10:29,433
{\an8}who's gonna tell us a little bit
about the, uh, toxic waste dumping

1321
01:10:29,516 --> 01:10:30,768
{\an8}at the Wallkill dump.

1322
01:10:31,477 --> 01:10:33,979
How are you doing?
My name is, uh, Armondo Bilancione,

1323
01:10:34,063 --> 01:10:36,106
and I work for the Orange County
Sheriff's Department.

1324
01:10:36,690 --> 01:10:41,487
My job, very simply, was to check
for toxic waste violations of the DEC laws

1325
01:10:41,570 --> 01:10:44,156
to the Orange County public landfill.

1326
01:10:44,240 --> 01:10:47,493
In the course of my duties
at that landfill,

1327
01:10:47,576 --> 01:10:49,119
I uncovered everything from toxic waste

1328
01:10:49,203 --> 01:10:51,872
to human body parts that local hospitals
dumped in that landfill

1329
01:10:51,956 --> 01:10:54,208
and mixed with regular garbage,
which is against the law.

1330
01:10:54,875 --> 01:10:58,128
We're talking syringes,
gauze soaked with blood,

1331
01:10:58,212 --> 01:11:01,257
organ pieces, and so forth.
I was like, you gotta be kidding me.

1332
01:11:01,340 --> 01:11:03,092
[chatting indistinctly]

1333
01:11:03,175 --> 01:11:07,054
[Armondo] To this day, toxic waste
is still buried in that landfill.

1334
01:11:07,137 --> 01:11:08,514
[gloomy music plays]

1335
01:11:08,597 --> 01:11:12,142
[boy 1] Got Chris, Nicky B, Mr. B…

1336
01:11:12,226 --> 01:11:14,853
-[student] Having fun.
-Big J. Cole representing.

1337
01:11:15,688 --> 01:11:18,274
-[Armondo] He found a broken syringe.
-[boy 2] Here, a syringe.

1338
01:11:18,357 --> 01:11:20,526
[Armondo] That is
a definite medical syringe.

1339
01:11:20,609 --> 01:11:22,361
[Rachel] You hold it just like-- Ooh.

1340
01:11:22,444 --> 01:11:24,196
In the beginning,
when I was at that landfill,

1341
01:11:24,280 --> 01:11:26,031
I was writing tickets
like an everyday affair.

1342
01:11:26,115 --> 01:11:28,409
Bam, bam, bam, bam.
Stop, get out of the truck.

1343
01:11:28,492 --> 01:11:30,953
Stop, get out of the truck.
And I was writing like a maniac.

1344
01:11:31,036 --> 01:11:33,664
I gave my tickets to my superiors
in the sheriff's department.

1345
01:11:33,747 --> 01:11:36,500
Tickets I wrote out to people
that were supposed to go to a court of law

1346
01:11:36,583 --> 01:11:38,168
never made it to the courthouses.

1347
01:11:38,252 --> 01:11:41,839
But at the landfill, we had a book
on the post, which was at post one.

1348
01:11:41,922 --> 01:11:43,007
Every ticket we issued,

1349
01:11:43,090 --> 01:11:46,218
everything that went down,
I wrote in that book.

1350
01:11:46,719 --> 01:11:49,680
Well, I've come to find out
that apparently,

1351
01:11:49,763 --> 01:11:52,433
there was a burglary at the landfill.

1352
01:11:53,434 --> 01:11:54,977
A burglary at the landfill.

1353
01:11:55,060 --> 01:11:59,023
I said, "Oh, my God, toxic waste
is never gonna be safe again."

1354
01:11:59,106 --> 01:12:00,691
Well, guess what they stole?

1355
01:12:00,774 --> 01:12:02,860
-[students] The book.
-Ha, ha!

1356
01:12:02,943 --> 01:12:04,361
They stole the book.

1357
01:12:04,445 --> 01:12:07,614
So what are we talking here, in essence?
You're talking, as far as I'm concerned,

1358
01:12:07,698 --> 01:12:09,408
and I-- I know I can prove it,

1359
01:12:09,491 --> 01:12:12,745
cover-ups from the county,
state, and federal level.

1360
01:12:12,828 --> 01:12:15,497
I implore you, I beg you,

1361
01:12:15,581 --> 01:12:17,541
I-- Truthfully, when I ask you
as a human being,

1362
01:12:17,624 --> 01:12:20,044
write to these people
under the Freedom of Information Act

1363
01:12:20,127 --> 01:12:21,503
and find out what they tell you.

1364
01:12:21,587 --> 01:12:24,006
[suspenseful music plays]

1365
01:12:24,089 --> 01:12:26,925
[Jeff] We knew all about
the Freedom of Information Act.

1366
01:12:28,135 --> 01:12:32,431
I mean, we bothered Bill Cummings
for dump records all the time.

1367
01:12:32,514 --> 01:12:35,768
It's crazy that that was
a part of our schooling.

1368
01:12:35,851 --> 01:12:38,645
[past Fred sniffs] Okay, guys.
We're rolling.

1369
01:12:38,729 --> 01:12:41,940
What happened the other day?
Why did you go to Mr. Cummings' office?

1370
01:12:42,524 --> 01:12:44,651
All right, we went there
because two weeks--

1371
01:12:44,735 --> 01:12:46,028
three weeks ago, actually,

1372
01:12:46,111 --> 01:12:48,364
we filled out
Freedom of Information requests

1373
01:12:48,447 --> 01:12:50,949
and they're supposed to come in
in ten working days.

1374
01:12:51,033 --> 01:12:54,286
And we never got the information,
so we went to see why we didn't get it.

1375
01:12:54,953 --> 01:12:57,164
At first, he just wanted
to, uh, get us out of there.

1376
01:12:57,247 --> 01:13:00,209
Then, at the end, he was frustrated
with us 'cause we were there so long.

1377
01:13:00,292 --> 01:13:02,878
He was like yelling.
He's got a foul mouth, too.

1378
01:13:02,961 --> 01:13:05,130
[David] Yeah. He was angry and defensive.

1379
01:13:06,465 --> 01:13:08,592
-[Fred] You say he had a foul mouth.
-Yeah.

1380
01:13:08,675 --> 01:13:10,844
[Fred] Nobody here minds.
What did he say?

1381
01:13:11,387 --> 01:13:14,056
Just, like, we asked him
if we can come in and look at the stuff,

1382
01:13:14,139 --> 01:13:16,141
and he said,
"Yeah, you can look at all that shit."

1383
01:13:17,309 --> 01:13:19,436
[Fred] You didn't think
he was gonna get violent on you?

1384
01:13:19,520 --> 01:13:20,604
He was angry, but--

1385
01:13:20,687 --> 01:13:22,981
-We were laughing when he started yelling.
-Not our problem.

1386
01:13:24,441 --> 01:13:27,694
{\an8}[Jeff] We took Mr. B's tip
and submitted a FOIA request

1387
01:13:27,778 --> 01:13:29,780
{\an8}to the Orange County Sheriff's Office.

1388
01:13:29,863 --> 01:13:32,658
{\an8}[past Fred] Uh, she said
there's a box waiting for us.

1389
01:13:32,741 --> 01:13:35,285
{\an8}[woman] A box waiting for you?
What's your name?

1390
01:13:35,369 --> 01:13:37,538
{\an8}[Fred] Isseks from Middletown High School.

1391
01:13:37,621 --> 01:13:39,540
{\an8}We get this box of stuff,

1392
01:13:39,623 --> 01:13:42,793
and there's all kinds
of papers in it, and it's there.

1393
01:13:42,876 --> 01:13:45,045
{\an8}-[past Fred] Uh, wow.
-[Rachel] We can close that door?

1394
01:13:45,129 --> 01:13:47,506
{\an8}[Fred] The log books. Holy moly!

1395
01:13:48,006 --> 01:13:49,383
{\an8}These are the log books.

1396
01:13:49,466 --> 01:13:51,218
{\an8}[Rachel] Sheriff's… notepad.

1397
01:13:51,301 --> 01:13:53,971
{\an8}-[Fred] This is it.
-[Rachel] This is it! [laughs]

1398
01:13:57,391 --> 01:13:59,977
{\an8}-[Rachel continues on video]
-[Jeff] It was wild.

1399
01:14:00,060 --> 01:14:02,187
{\an8}Real-time evidence of toxic dumping

1400
01:14:02,271 --> 01:14:05,232
{\an8}and proof of the parties
that were responsible for it.

1401
01:14:05,899 --> 01:14:09,111
{\an8}-[boy] Look at all these pictures.
-[Rachel] These are the truck drivers!

1402
01:14:10,529 --> 01:14:12,781
{\an8}[past Fred] Does it say
anything about tickets?

1403
01:14:14,116 --> 01:14:15,367
[Jeff] So then we took

1404
01:14:15,451 --> 01:14:17,327
all that information
to the district attorney

1405
01:14:17,411 --> 01:14:20,539
to see if there were
any charges actually filed.

1406
01:14:20,622 --> 01:14:23,333
-[past Rachel] That's what I said.
-[past Fred] Where's the letter "A"?

1407
01:14:24,793 --> 01:14:27,671
-[Rachel] Probably in the beginning.
-[past Fred] Okay, I have a feeling.

1408
01:14:28,380 --> 01:14:31,717
[Rachel] Not there.
It's not in that one, look in this one.

1409
01:14:32,718 --> 01:14:33,719
No, those aren't…

1410
01:14:33,802 --> 01:14:36,346
[Fred] We suspected that people in power

1411
01:14:36,430 --> 01:14:39,349
were all involved in some way
in squashing those tickets

1412
01:14:39,433 --> 01:14:41,685
because they never saw the light of day.

1413
01:14:41,768 --> 01:14:44,062
[woman] Um, excuse me,
I don't think that you can…

1414
01:14:45,147 --> 01:14:46,106
Excuse me!

1415
01:14:46,190 --> 01:14:48,484
Your tape…
I don't know what you've got on it,

1416
01:14:48,567 --> 01:14:50,319
but you're going to have to leave it.

1417
01:14:50,402 --> 01:14:52,988
-[girl] Why?
-[Rachel] Isn't it public information?

1418
01:14:53,071 --> 01:14:55,073
[woman] Yeah, but you can't film it!

1419
01:15:00,746 --> 01:15:01,663
[Armondo] At one time,

1420
01:15:01,747 --> 01:15:03,916
I had a verbal argument
with a driver of a truck.

1421
01:15:03,999 --> 01:15:07,336
I couldn't believe the name of this person

1422
01:15:07,419 --> 01:15:09,046
that came out of the truck,

1423
01:15:09,755 --> 01:15:13,050
which was, um, very Italian, shall we say.

1424
01:15:13,592 --> 01:15:15,302
And, uh, I issued him a ticket.

1425
01:15:15,385 --> 01:15:17,346
And when I found
that he didn't have a permit,

1426
01:15:18,847 --> 01:15:20,557
I just knew something was wrong here.

1427
01:15:22,267 --> 01:15:26,230
I was told by certain drivers
that, uh, I was pissing off the people

1428
01:15:26,313 --> 01:15:28,232
that own Round Lake Sanitation.

1429
01:15:28,857 --> 01:15:31,026
It was public knowledge
Round Lake Sanitation,

1430
01:15:31,109 --> 01:15:33,862
which was the biggest garbage company
in Orange County,

1431
01:15:33,946 --> 01:15:36,156
was in fact run by organized crime.

1432
01:15:36,657 --> 01:15:38,659
So one day a black,
I think it was a Cadillac

1433
01:15:38,742 --> 01:15:41,203
and a Mercedes pull up,
and all these Italian guys,

1434
01:15:41,286 --> 01:15:43,789
they smell like garlic and stuff--
And I'm Italian.

1435
01:15:43,872 --> 01:15:46,375
They come out,
and, "Who is this guy, Bilancione?"

1436
01:15:46,458 --> 01:15:48,794
I said, "Oh, here we go.
It's the Guido Sarduccis," you know?

1437
01:15:48,877 --> 01:15:51,421
Bad enough they're in Brooklyn,
now they're up here, you know?

1438
01:15:51,505 --> 01:15:52,464
Only kidding.

1439
01:15:52,548 --> 01:15:55,300
A couple of them, I believe it was
Mr. Mongelli or his son,

1440
01:15:55,384 --> 01:15:56,510
exchanged some words with me,

1441
01:15:56,593 --> 01:15:58,929
Very simply, I told him,
"All I'm doing is my job."

1442
01:15:59,012 --> 01:16:00,847
"And I will continue to do my job."

1443
01:16:02,266 --> 01:16:05,435
[Fred] I didn't know much
about the Mongellis at all.

1444
01:16:05,519 --> 01:16:09,189
I remember mentioning it to my mother,
who said, "Oh, I know them."

1445
01:16:09,273 --> 01:16:13,068
Uh, "Their children went to school
in my school when I was a principal."

1446
01:16:13,151 --> 01:16:14,528
"Very nice people."

1447
01:16:14,611 --> 01:16:16,780
"When, uh, they had parents' night,

1448
01:16:16,863 --> 01:16:19,658
Mr. Mongelli would come in
and look after his kids."

1449
01:16:19,741 --> 01:16:21,743
[ominous music plays]

1450
01:16:22,536 --> 01:16:25,330
It seemed like something
out of a bad movie.

1451
01:16:27,499 --> 01:16:29,001
[man 1] Garbage is garbage.

1452
01:16:29,084 --> 01:16:32,588
[man 2] No, not when you're servicing
Fortune 500 companies like this.

1453
01:16:33,380 --> 01:16:35,299
Talking three-quarter,
maybe a billion metric tons

1454
01:16:35,382 --> 01:16:38,677
of waste in this country each year.
That's big bucks, Harold.

1455
01:16:38,760 --> 01:16:40,345
You have no idea how big.

1456
01:16:41,972 --> 01:16:46,935
We're talking about mob-controlled,
Mafia-connected carting companies

1457
01:16:47,019 --> 01:16:49,229
and gangsters that were doing the dumping,

1458
01:16:49,313 --> 01:16:51,607
and they just happened to be
in our neighborhood.

1459
01:16:51,690 --> 01:16:53,275
[dramatic cinematic music plays]

1460
01:16:55,819 --> 01:16:59,031
I was told that there's
a $5,000 bounty on my head,

1461
01:16:59,114 --> 01:17:01,491
that the first driver
that runs me over gets it.

1462
01:17:01,575 --> 01:17:04,411
{\an8}And I don't mean run me over
to give me a broken toenail, no, no, no.

1463
01:17:04,494 --> 01:17:06,788
It was to bury me with the waste,
there's no doubt.

1464
01:17:06,872 --> 01:17:08,373
I don't know if Mr. Isseks knows this--

1465
01:17:08,457 --> 01:17:10,709
[on PA] <i>Pardon the interruption.</i>
<i>We have one brief announcement.</i>

1466
01:17:10,792 --> 01:17:13,712
<i>The wrestling cheerleading practice</i>
<i>for today is cancelled.</i>

1467
01:17:13,795 --> 01:17:16,256
<i>There is a home meet tomorrow.</i>

1468
01:17:18,800 --> 01:17:22,512
[Fred] <i>Mr. B told us he knew</i>
<i>of another potential whistleblower</i>

1469
01:17:22,596 --> 01:17:24,056
<i>from the landfill.</i>

1470
01:17:24,139 --> 01:17:26,808
{\an8}<i>He was willing to let us</i>
<i>interview him in my kitchen,</i>

1471
01:17:26,892 --> 01:17:28,852
{\an8}<i>but he wanted a lawyer present.</i>

1472
01:17:28,935 --> 01:17:30,604
{\an8}Yeah. Is somebody gonna ask questions?

1473
01:17:30,687 --> 01:17:34,066
[Fred] Were there occasions when a car
would pull up after the gate was closed?

1474
01:17:34,149 --> 01:17:35,359
Oh, yeah. Uh…

1475
01:17:35,442 --> 01:17:37,444
[Fred] Would you describe
that story please?

1476
01:17:37,527 --> 01:17:40,238
Um, my understanding was, uh…

1477
01:17:40,864 --> 01:17:42,866
it was, uh, Lou Mongelli.

1478
01:17:43,867 --> 01:17:44,785
Hold on a minute.

1479
01:17:44,868 --> 01:17:47,621
[Armondo] Relax. You are among friends.

1480
01:17:47,704 --> 01:17:50,999
[Fred reading]

1481
01:17:51,083 --> 01:17:53,085
<i>…but for totally different reasons.</i>

1482
01:17:53,168 --> 01:17:56,797
<i>Dieter wants his lawyer to see</i>
<i>the script for the documentary</i>

1483
01:17:56,880 --> 01:17:58,590
<i>to see if Dieter should back out of it.</i>

1484
01:17:58,674 --> 01:18:00,967
Good morning, how are you doing?
Mondo here.

1485
01:18:01,051 --> 01:18:02,803
[Fred] <i>Mr. B, on the other hand,</i>

1486
01:18:02,886 --> 01:18:06,348
<i>wants to know when "our" documentary</i>
<i>is going to be done.</i>

1487
01:18:06,431 --> 01:18:07,808
<i>Everything is "we,"</i>

1488
01:18:07,891 --> 01:18:11,728
<i>especially the "we"</i>
<i>in "we are going to be rich after this."</i>

1489
01:18:11,812 --> 01:18:12,771
Unbelievable.

1490
01:18:12,854 --> 01:18:16,900
[director] Did you ever think
to yourself, "Is this guy credible?"

1491
01:18:16,983 --> 01:18:18,985
Mm-hmm. Yeah.

1492
01:18:19,069 --> 01:18:21,613
It was easy to dismiss Armondo as,

1493
01:18:21,697 --> 01:18:24,700
"Ah, he's just a whacked-out dude
who wants to be tough."

1494
01:18:24,783 --> 01:18:28,412
I believe he was also, uh,
an aspiring actor at the time.

1495
01:18:29,162 --> 01:18:31,164
{\an8}[dramatic music plays]

1496
01:18:33,041 --> 01:18:34,251
Take a look at this.

1497
01:18:34,960 --> 01:18:36,253
Touch it!

1498
01:18:36,878 --> 01:18:38,547
Go on, touch it!

1499
01:18:39,548 --> 01:18:42,384
[laughs menacingly]

1500
01:18:42,968 --> 01:18:45,429
[Mike] I think
we were savvy enough to recognize

1501
01:18:45,512 --> 01:18:48,140
Mr. B's limitations in presentation.

1502
01:18:50,016 --> 01:18:51,977
[Fred] We needed corroboration.

1503
01:18:52,811 --> 01:18:55,397
[past Jeff] In the past year,
we've learned that the Mongellis

1504
01:18:55,480 --> 01:18:59,651
has had control of the dumping,
and there are all these toxic waste sites.

1505
01:18:59,735 --> 01:19:03,739
{\an8}Wouldn't a little common sense
tell us that the mob was involved,

1506
01:19:03,822 --> 01:19:07,743
{\an8}and that they needed
the cooperation of the town

1507
01:19:07,826 --> 01:19:10,912
{\an8}and legislatures? "Legislators."

1508
01:19:11,413 --> 01:19:13,582
Who's going to start our cars for us?

1509
01:19:14,124 --> 01:19:15,834
[laughs]

1510
01:19:15,917 --> 01:19:18,712
Let-- Let me just say
that we've had a lot of things

1511
01:19:18,795 --> 01:19:21,339
go on in Orange County, in many landfills.

1512
01:19:22,174 --> 01:19:24,968
A man was killed at the Tuxedo dump

1513
01:19:25,051 --> 01:19:28,972
because he had information
that other people did not want out,

1514
01:19:29,055 --> 01:19:32,309
and he got buried in another dump
over in the town of Montgomery.

1515
01:19:34,603 --> 01:19:35,604
-Hi.
-Hi.

1516
01:19:37,981 --> 01:19:40,317
Thank you for coming here.
We appreciate this very much.

1517
01:19:40,400 --> 01:19:41,693
We know you'll help us out a lot.

1518
01:19:42,277 --> 01:19:45,197
Do you feel that the past boards
have made money off of this,

1519
01:19:45,280 --> 01:19:46,406
off of this dumping?

1520
01:19:46,490 --> 01:19:47,824
In my investigations,

1521
01:19:47,908 --> 01:19:50,660
I've, uh, uncovered a number of instances

1522
01:19:50,744 --> 01:19:53,663
where members of town boards
received bribes

1523
01:19:53,747 --> 01:19:58,794
from, uh, carters who were working
for organized crime

1524
01:19:58,877 --> 01:20:00,629
or who were controlled by organized crime.

1525
01:20:00,712 --> 01:20:03,340
[sparse intriguing music plays]

1526
01:20:03,423 --> 01:20:06,593
Um, I took, uh, the materials that we had,

1527
01:20:06,676 --> 01:20:09,054
and we did extensive investigations,
as you know.

1528
01:20:10,889 --> 01:20:13,934
Here is a copy of our report entitled,

1529
01:20:14,017 --> 01:20:16,812
"Organized Crime's Involvement
in the Waste Hauling Industry,"

1530
01:20:16,895 --> 01:20:20,440
uh, which documents
and names specific people

1531
01:20:20,524 --> 01:20:24,110
who were involved
in these-- in these very serious crimes.

1532
01:20:25,195 --> 01:20:26,530
[Fred] Up until that point,

1533
01:20:26,613 --> 01:20:29,741
the Mongellis were an abstraction
to me, pretty much.

1534
01:20:29,825 --> 01:20:32,494
But it was Hinchey's report
that kind of colored it in,

1535
01:20:32,577 --> 01:20:33,495
what was going on.

1536
01:20:36,748 --> 01:20:40,502
It's a family tree of organized crime
in the carting industry.

1537
01:20:41,002 --> 01:20:44,214
My eyes kind of went
to the companies and individuals

1538
01:20:44,297 --> 01:20:45,757
that I had heard of.

1539
01:20:47,884 --> 01:20:50,512
And I realized that we were
just kind of scraping

1540
01:20:50,595 --> 01:20:53,181
a little corner of the entire story.

1541
01:20:54,933 --> 01:20:57,894
[Hinchey] Thousands and thousands
of tons of materials

1542
01:20:57,978 --> 01:20:59,813
were dumped throughout the Hudson Valley

1543
01:20:59,896 --> 01:21:02,816
under the direction
of organized crime figures.

1544
01:21:04,025 --> 01:21:08,154
And, in fact,
you can find these sites, um,

1545
01:21:08,238 --> 01:21:12,158
generally within a 200-mile radius
of New York City.

1546
01:21:13,618 --> 01:21:15,579
{\an8}So in addition to upstate New York,

1547
01:21:15,662 --> 01:21:18,582
{\an8}you could also find them in parts
of New England, and in Pennsylvania,

1548
01:21:18,665 --> 01:21:22,210
{\an8}and in New Jersey,
and elsewhere in the immediate region.

1549
01:21:22,294 --> 01:21:23,879
{\an8}It was compelling because,

1550
01:21:23,962 --> 01:21:27,549
{\an8}wow, that's the kind of thing
that happens in movies and in books.

1551
01:21:27,632 --> 01:21:29,092
{\an8}It doesn't happen in your backyard.

1552
01:21:29,175 --> 01:21:34,973
So when we started using the "M" word,
"Mafia," it became the hook.

1553
01:21:35,056 --> 01:21:38,852
Stick around. When we return,
teenagers take on a real-life project

1554
01:21:38,935 --> 01:21:41,521
exposing corruption and the mob.

1555
01:21:41,605 --> 01:21:44,983
{\an8}Growing up as a kid, I just felt
like, you know, government would--

1556
01:21:45,066 --> 01:21:46,276
{\an8}you know, was there to help us.

1557
01:21:46,359 --> 01:21:48,153
{\an8}Then when I started
getting involved with this,

1558
01:21:48,236 --> 01:21:52,449
{\an8}I realized that, you know, government
was just a lot of dealmaking

1559
01:21:52,532 --> 01:21:54,284
and stuff going on behind closed doors.

1560
01:21:54,367 --> 01:21:58,371
Didn't that frighten you guys, knowing
that there was a mob connection here?

1561
01:21:58,455 --> 01:22:00,999
Well, when it became clear
that the mob was involved,

1562
01:22:01,082 --> 01:22:04,836
it just sort of made us say,
"Well, that explains a lot."

1563
01:22:04,920 --> 01:22:07,964
[news anchor] Maurice Hinchey was offered
a quarter of a million dollars

1564
01:22:08,048 --> 01:22:11,551
as a bribe to end the investigation
into the dirty dumping.

1565
01:22:11,635 --> 01:22:14,471
But instead of accepting the bribe,
Hinchey would turn evidence

1566
01:22:14,554 --> 01:22:17,140
that would help put
the Mongelli brothers behind bars.

1567
01:22:17,223 --> 01:22:18,516
[Fred] He was wired.

1568
01:22:18,600 --> 01:22:20,101
He got that on tape.

1569
01:22:20,685 --> 01:22:22,354
And it became part of the story.

1570
01:22:22,437 --> 01:22:25,065
Are you left with a sense
that you affected real change

1571
01:22:25,148 --> 01:22:27,233
and the situation
has been brought to a close?

1572
01:22:27,317 --> 01:22:30,070
Well, we still have the sense
that we've got more work to do.

1573
01:22:31,738 --> 01:22:37,035
[man] Today, you're experiencing
what we experienced back then.

1574
01:22:37,118 --> 01:22:39,287
{\an8}The wise guys are still running it.

1575
01:22:39,371 --> 01:22:42,999
{\an8}They're still putting
anything imaginable in it.

1576
01:22:43,083 --> 01:22:44,668
They couldn't care less.

1577
01:22:45,543 --> 01:22:48,338
Garbage isn't just
what your mother tells you to take out.

1578
01:22:48,421 --> 01:22:49,923
It's a big, big business.

1579
01:22:50,840 --> 01:22:53,593
Our saying when we were working,
is "garbage is gold."

1580
01:22:53,677 --> 01:22:56,054
And it is. Garbage is gold.

1581
01:22:56,930 --> 01:23:00,934
Do we have anything to fear
with what we're doing?

1582
01:23:01,017 --> 01:23:03,478
Do you think that anyone
would come after us?

1583
01:23:04,562 --> 01:23:05,689
Don't be foolish.

1584
01:23:05,772 --> 01:23:10,235
Don't… wander around by yourselves
in landfills or anything like that.

1585
01:23:11,945 --> 01:23:13,947
[telephone rings]

1586
01:23:15,323 --> 01:23:18,493
[Fred] <i>1:00 a.m., an anonymous phone call.</i>

1587
01:23:19,119 --> 01:23:22,497
[Fred reading]

1588
01:23:22,580 --> 01:23:24,666
<i>You've had your 15 minutes of fame.</i>

1589
01:23:24,749 --> 01:23:26,167
<i>Now let it go.</i>

1590
01:23:26,918 --> 01:23:28,420
[ominous music plays]

1591
01:23:28,503 --> 01:23:30,755
Weird things would start happening.

1592
01:23:32,007 --> 01:23:33,341
You'd pick up the telephone,

1593
01:23:33,425 --> 01:23:37,387
and this is the era of, like,
the house phone with the long cord,

1594
01:23:37,470 --> 01:23:40,223
and you'd hear a clicking,
like a weird… [mimics clicking]

1595
01:23:40,306 --> 01:23:42,142
Then, I'm like,
"I think our phones are tapped."

1596
01:23:42,225 --> 01:23:43,768
Like, "I think they're fucking with us."

1597
01:23:43,852 --> 01:23:44,811
Maurice?

1598
01:23:44,894 --> 01:23:47,022
My ma says
you're gonna get bumped off, man.

1599
01:23:47,105 --> 01:23:49,024
[students chuckle]

1600
01:23:49,107 --> 01:23:51,026
-By your mom?
-[students chuckle]

1601
01:23:51,109 --> 01:23:53,111
[students chatter excitedly]

1602
01:23:54,404 --> 01:23:56,865
[boy] Yeah! Bonfire!

1603
01:23:56,948 --> 01:23:58,950
[cheering]

1604
01:24:01,036 --> 01:24:03,371
[Jeff] There was a guy at a party,

1605
01:24:03,455 --> 01:24:07,000
and he was only
a couple years older than me,

1606
01:24:07,083 --> 01:24:10,086
but he did threaten me,

1607
01:24:10,170 --> 01:24:15,633
uh, and told me
to stay away from the landfill.

1608
01:24:15,717 --> 01:24:19,971
Exposing them to all of that,
it was at times a conflict for me,

1609
01:24:20,055 --> 01:24:22,932
and I think it was even more of a conflict
with my own daughter,

1610
01:24:23,600 --> 01:24:28,021
in that she was younger
than my students at the time,

1611
01:24:28,104 --> 01:24:30,398
and I was bringing
some of this stuff home,

1612
01:24:30,482 --> 01:24:32,901
and I was talking candidly
with Denise about it,

1613
01:24:32,984 --> 01:24:33,902
and Sadie would listen.

1614
01:24:33,985 --> 01:24:38,073
And I'm thinking, "Wow, I'm really gonna
turn her off to the whole world

1615
01:24:38,156 --> 01:24:41,284
by-- by exposing her
to all of this stuff."

1616
01:24:41,367 --> 01:24:44,245
[director] Do you remember
any student saying to you,

1617
01:24:44,329 --> 01:24:49,626
"Mmm, Mr. Isseks, ah,
it's just getting too weird, too freaky"?

1618
01:24:51,294 --> 01:24:52,670
No. No.

1619
01:24:53,254 --> 01:24:54,297
It's like pretty colors.

1620
01:24:54,380 --> 01:24:55,840
[students chatter and laugh]

1621
01:24:57,759 --> 01:25:00,470
[Fred] Electronic English was an elective.

1622
01:25:00,553 --> 01:25:04,057
If this kind of thing
really turns you off,

1623
01:25:04,724 --> 01:25:07,727
{\an8}take modern literature, take Shakespeare,

1624
01:25:07,811 --> 01:25:09,270
{\an8}take creative writing.

1625
01:25:09,354 --> 01:25:11,147
{\an8}Don't take Electronic English,

1626
01:25:11,231 --> 01:25:13,608
{\an8}'cause you know
this is gonna be part of it.

1627
01:25:15,318 --> 01:25:17,570
[gentle classical piano music plays]

1628
01:25:24,702 --> 01:25:28,581
[past Fred] <i>Hi, this is Fred Isseks</i>
<i>for School Radio here on 1340 Wall,</i>

1629
01:25:28,665 --> 01:25:31,417
<i>sitting with a number</i>
<i>of Middletown High School seniors</i>

1630
01:25:31,501 --> 01:25:34,712
<i>right on the verge of graduating</i>
<i>in just a few months.</i>

1631
01:25:34,796 --> 01:25:37,132
<i>Are you looking forward</i>
<i>to graduating from high school?</i>

1632
01:25:37,215 --> 01:25:40,885
[boy] <i>I think it would be nice</i>
<i>to finally go out on my own, and…</i>

1633
01:25:40,969 --> 01:25:43,847
<i>and I know I could live well,</i>
<i>independently as an adult.</i>

1634
01:25:43,930 --> 01:25:46,141
<i>And I wanna prove that to myself</i>
<i>as well as my parents.</i>

1635
01:25:46,224 --> 01:25:47,684
[past Fred] <i>How about somebody else?</i>

1636
01:25:47,767 --> 01:25:50,061
<i>Rachel, I believe you're a senior,</i>
<i>is that correct?</i>

1637
01:25:50,145 --> 01:25:51,771
[Rachel] <i>Yes, I'm a senior.</i>

1638
01:25:51,855 --> 01:25:53,356
[Fred] <i>Looking forward to getting out?</i>

1639
01:25:53,439 --> 01:25:56,067
[Rachel] <i>Yeah, I can't wait to graduate.</i>
<i>I can't wait for the future.</i>

1640
01:25:56,151 --> 01:26:02,198
<i>I'm planning to move out to Los Angeles</i>
<i>and, uh, go to film school at USC.</i>

1641
01:26:02,282 --> 01:26:05,201
[Fred] <i>Jeff, what about you?</i>
<i>Any plans for when you get out of school?</i>

1642
01:26:05,827 --> 01:26:08,496
[Jeff] <i>Well, um, I haven't really</i>
<i>thought much about it.</i>

1643
01:26:08,580 --> 01:26:12,542
<i>I still have a few years to go</i>
<i>until I get out of here.</i>

1644
01:26:12,625 --> 01:26:15,587
[Fred] <i>Does the thought</i>
<i>of leaving high school inspire you</i>

1645
01:26:15,670 --> 01:26:17,005
<i>or does it fill you with fear?</i>

1646
01:26:18,256 --> 01:26:22,468
[Jeff] <i>It doesn't inspire me,</i>
<i>but it doesn't fill me with fear either.</i>

1647
01:26:22,552 --> 01:26:24,721
<i>Um, I just want to get out.</i>

1648
01:26:24,804 --> 01:26:26,514
[serene ethereal music plays]

1649
01:26:29,392 --> 01:26:31,060
[David] By the time I was a senior,

1650
01:26:31,144 --> 01:26:34,564
I ended up taking Fred's classes,
I believe, five times,

1651
01:26:34,647 --> 01:26:37,775
because I would just naturally
always wanna spend more time in the studio

1652
01:26:37,859 --> 01:26:38,985
or be around more.

1653
01:26:39,068 --> 01:26:41,321
People are starting
to talk about this issue.

1654
01:26:41,404 --> 01:26:44,240
They're starting to ask
good questions about the cap,

1655
01:26:44,324 --> 01:26:47,577
and who's putting it in,
and how much control the state has on it.

1656
01:26:48,494 --> 01:26:51,247
[David] I hate to say it,
but I know there was a perception

1657
01:26:51,331 --> 01:26:55,084
that Fred was sort of leading us
into danger,

1658
01:26:55,627 --> 01:26:59,172
and that this had turned
into this quixotic quest.

1659
01:27:00,173 --> 01:27:02,508
[Sadie] Let's say hi to Daddy.

1660
01:27:02,592 --> 01:27:03,843
See how he's doing.

1661
01:27:03,927 --> 01:27:06,012
Daddy, get off the phone now!
[blows raspberry]

1662
01:27:06,095 --> 01:27:07,972
-Oh, Jesus.
-[Sadie laughs]

1663
01:27:11,726 --> 01:27:15,355
[Rachel] You know, it's hard to say
with clarity what Fred even hoped

1664
01:27:15,438 --> 01:27:17,273
the endgame would be.

1665
01:27:17,982 --> 01:27:21,778
I personally hoped
people would see what we did

1666
01:27:21,861 --> 01:27:25,323
and care and stop.

1667
01:27:25,406 --> 01:27:29,202
Like, do clean that landfill out,

1668
01:27:29,285 --> 01:27:31,287
not just put a cap on it.

1669
01:27:32,205 --> 01:27:35,416
But, you know, then I grew up
and I went to college and I left,

1670
01:27:35,500 --> 01:27:37,710
and the story continued after me.

1671
01:27:37,794 --> 01:27:39,796
["Dirty Water" by Throwing Muses plays]

1672
01:27:42,006 --> 01:27:45,176
[past Fred] I'm thinking
we hire a plane for the day.

1673
01:27:47,762 --> 01:27:51,432
If we get lots of toxic waste on camera,

1674
01:27:51,516 --> 01:27:53,810
then that makes liars out of all of them.

1675
01:27:55,937 --> 01:28:00,191
{\an8}Okay. You got a list of questions
that you wanted to ask me? Very good.

1676
01:28:00,275 --> 01:28:04,112
[girl] We're here in reference
to the illegal dumping that was going on.

1677
01:28:04,195 --> 01:28:05,738
Okay, let me stop you right here.

1678
01:28:05,822 --> 01:28:08,449
I let you come in here
to talk to me about Earth Day.

1679
01:28:08,533 --> 01:28:10,868
I don't have the time to get into this.

1680
01:28:11,411 --> 01:28:16,833
<i>♪ Look at the dirty water</i>
<i>Look at him… ♪</i>

1681
01:28:16,916 --> 01:28:19,294
It seems to be
that you're more of a crusader

1682
01:28:19,377 --> 01:28:21,379
rather than a journalist,

1683
01:28:21,462 --> 01:28:23,673
and I wanted to know
how you would react to that.

1684
01:28:25,383 --> 01:28:26,801
That's a tough one.

1685
01:28:26,884 --> 01:28:30,763
When we started, it was pure journalism,
trying to-- to get at the truth.

1686
01:28:30,847 --> 01:28:32,223
And then as we worked on it,

1687
01:28:32,307 --> 01:28:35,143
it became clear that there was
a horrible thing going on here.

1688
01:28:35,226 --> 01:28:38,396
So in the sense
that I want to see a correction,

1689
01:28:38,479 --> 01:28:41,232
I would like to see the water
protected better than it is,

1690
01:28:41,316 --> 01:28:43,484
I guess in that way,
I am a crusader of sorts.

1691
01:28:43,568 --> 01:28:47,155
<i>♪ Sit at your hole… ♪</i>

1692
01:28:47,989 --> 01:28:49,574
He's got a lot of hubbub.

1693
01:28:51,242 --> 01:28:53,202
{\an8}[Fred] We've gotta get--
You gotta talk to him.

1694
01:28:53,286 --> 01:28:55,371
{\an8}-[boy] All right.
-[Fred] Tell him what we're doing.

1695
01:28:55,455 --> 01:28:56,748
[boy] All right.

1696
01:28:58,583 --> 01:28:59,500
Sir?

1697
01:29:00,960 --> 01:29:02,086
Sir?

1698
01:29:02,170 --> 01:29:04,464
The county executives
can tell you all about it,

1699
01:29:04,547 --> 01:29:06,632
what we've done at the high school
as far as the tape.

1700
01:29:06,716 --> 01:29:08,009
Good for you!

1701
01:29:13,056 --> 01:29:15,516
[past Fred] Go ahead, Santos.
I got you covered.

1702
01:29:16,768 --> 01:29:20,063
There's a place supposedly
where the fence is low enough to jump.

1703
01:29:23,441 --> 01:29:24,984
What's this letter?

1704
01:29:28,112 --> 01:29:28,946
Ah!

1705
01:29:31,449 --> 01:29:33,034
[Fred reads]

1706
01:29:33,576 --> 01:29:36,120
"You deliberately led
your students to trespass

1707
01:29:36,204 --> 01:29:39,665
{\an8}in a restricted maintenance area
on county property."

1708
01:29:39,749 --> 01:29:42,585
{\an8}"More troublesome, however,
is how facile you are

1709
01:29:42,668 --> 01:29:46,923
{\an8}in your deceit of innocence
in happening upon this site."

1710
01:29:47,006 --> 01:29:49,967
{\an8}"This is a terrible attribute
to be teaching your students."

1711
01:29:50,051 --> 01:29:54,806
{\an8}"Rest assured that future trespasses
will be dealt with appropriately."

1712
01:30:04,690 --> 01:30:07,944
{\an8}I remember I was in the media center
kind of hanging out,

1713
01:30:08,027 --> 01:30:09,612
and Fred came over.

1714
01:30:09,695 --> 01:30:13,032
And I said, "Hey, how's it going?"
And he said, "Uh, not so great."

1715
01:30:13,866 --> 01:30:17,662
The district had contacted him
and said the school had had enough.

1716
01:30:17,745 --> 01:30:20,289
"We're gonna draw a line in the sand.
You're gonna stop doing this,

1717
01:30:20,373 --> 01:30:23,126
especially with the students,
especially with school equipment."

1718
01:30:23,209 --> 01:30:26,045
"And if for any reason
you do not abide by this,

1719
01:30:26,129 --> 01:30:29,632
you-- you will ultimately
not work here anymore."

1720
01:30:30,216 --> 01:30:32,009
And I-I-I'll never forget it.

1721
01:30:32,093 --> 01:30:33,261
And Fred said, "Yeah."

1722
01:30:33,344 --> 01:30:37,014
"So I was, you know,
on, like, my third Heineken last night

1723
01:30:37,098 --> 01:30:39,142
and I was thinking about this."

1724
01:30:39,225 --> 01:30:42,311
"I realized I don't need
this job that bad."

1725
01:30:42,895 --> 01:30:45,148
And I was like, "Fuck, yeah!"

1726
01:30:45,731 --> 01:30:48,985
-[man] Excuse me. You need help, guys?
-[Fred] We're from Middletown High School.

1727
01:30:49,068 --> 01:30:50,486
-How's it going?
-[Fred] Good.

1728
01:30:50,570 --> 01:30:52,738
We wanted to videotape
how the cap is going.

1729
01:30:52,822 --> 01:30:54,699
Is it okay if we just
sort of cruise around here?

1730
01:30:54,782 --> 01:30:55,783
Actually, no.

1731
01:30:55,867 --> 01:31:01,497
Unfortunately, you guys aren't
really dressed to go through the landfill.

1732
01:31:02,081 --> 01:31:05,460
And, uh, you're not…
You're gonna stop doing that, right?

1733
01:31:05,543 --> 01:31:08,045
-[Mike] Huh?
-You're gonna stop videotaping me?

1734
01:31:08,129 --> 01:31:09,630
-I appreciate that.
-[Fred] I'm sorry.

1735
01:31:11,424 --> 01:31:15,011
[Mike] These are the, uh…
the liners, I think.

1736
01:31:15,845 --> 01:31:17,305
<i>Ah, shit. Truck's coming at us.</i>

1737
01:31:17,388 --> 01:31:18,473
[laughs]

1738
01:31:19,807 --> 01:31:21,517
[Mike] <i>Can you get that right there?</i>

1739
01:31:21,601 --> 01:31:23,561
Now I'm using my house key

1740
01:31:23,644 --> 01:31:26,731
and I'm cutting
right through the actual liner.

1741
01:31:26,814 --> 01:31:28,524
This is the actual lining.

1742
01:31:28,608 --> 01:31:31,569
I'm actually using my house key to cut it.

1743
01:31:31,652 --> 01:31:33,029
You'll see that in a second, okay?

1744
01:31:37,158 --> 01:31:38,201
[chuckles]

1745
01:31:38,784 --> 01:31:40,203
<i>It's just my house key.</i>

1746
01:31:40,953 --> 01:31:42,830
<i>And I'm cutting a piece right off of here.</i>

1747
01:31:42,914 --> 01:31:43,956
[laughs]

1748
01:31:44,040 --> 01:31:45,917
<i>-And it's cutting…</i>
-Who's that kid, man?

1749
01:31:46,000 --> 01:31:47,502
<i>Not too easily, but it's cutting.</i>

1750
01:31:47,585 --> 01:31:49,712
[Mike] It felt good to graduate
and leave this behind.

1751
01:31:49,795 --> 01:31:51,506
Because it took growth to move on

1752
01:31:51,589 --> 01:31:54,634
and leave it to the next generation
of kids who were gonna work on it.

1753
01:31:54,717 --> 01:31:58,054
The company that the town and the state
decided to hire to cap the landfill

1754
01:31:58,137 --> 01:31:59,305
has a bad record.

1755
01:31:59,388 --> 01:32:01,766
New York City refused to hire
the Tully Corporation

1756
01:32:01,849 --> 01:32:04,727
because of allegations
that they were linked to organized crime.

1757
01:32:05,478 --> 01:32:10,566
Did you know that, uh, Tully was connected
to organized crime in New York City?

1758
01:32:10,650 --> 01:32:12,652
{\an8}I'm not saying they're not linked
to organized crime.

1759
01:32:12,735 --> 01:32:15,655
{\an8}I'm not saying they are.
I'm saying it didn't matter to us,

1760
01:32:15,738 --> 01:32:16,781
frankly, okay?

1761
01:32:16,864 --> 01:32:18,407
Our legal obligation,

1762
01:32:18,491 --> 01:32:20,993
and I think our moral obligation
to do what's right

1763
01:32:21,077 --> 01:32:22,662
by our taxpayers and our residents,

1764
01:32:22,745 --> 01:32:26,040
was to award
to the lowest qualified bidder.

1765
01:32:26,123 --> 01:32:27,583
And that's the end of the story.

1766
01:32:28,334 --> 01:32:30,336
[David] <i>60 Minutes </i>came to town,

1767
01:32:30,419 --> 01:32:34,590
the <i>New York Times, ABC World News.</i>

1768
01:32:34,674 --> 01:32:39,011
And it absolutely felt like, finally,

1769
01:32:39,679 --> 01:32:44,892
now we've finally gone up the ladder,
and now we'll see, um, some action.

1770
01:32:44,976 --> 01:32:46,227
Well, in other news tonight,

1771
01:32:46,310 --> 01:32:49,272
Wallkill officials will have
some special guests

1772
01:32:49,355 --> 01:32:51,774
at their town board meeting
coming up tonight.

1773
01:32:51,857 --> 01:32:55,403
Tonight, Fred Isseks' class will attend
a town board meeting in Wallkill,

1774
01:32:55,486 --> 01:32:57,863
as they have many times
in the past four years.

1775
01:32:57,947 --> 01:33:00,616
{\an8}The difference is
the national media will be present.

1776
01:33:00,700 --> 01:33:02,702
[townspeople chatter]

1777
01:33:04,996 --> 01:33:08,040
[man] Before we open it up to the public,
uh, tonight we have a lot of guests.

1778
01:33:08,124 --> 01:33:10,293
We have a lot of, uh, media
here this evening.

1779
01:33:10,376 --> 01:33:13,087
Welcome to everyone
to the town of Wallkill.

1780
01:33:13,170 --> 01:33:17,592
Uh, I'd like to make a brief statement
about, uh, the media exposure

1781
01:33:17,675 --> 01:33:20,553
which the town of Wallkill Landfill
has recently received.

1782
01:33:20,636 --> 01:33:24,432
In order to ensure that this coverage
does not confuse the facts,

1783
01:33:24,515 --> 01:33:26,892
I would like to emphasize
the following points.

1784
01:33:27,768 --> 01:33:31,230
The Middletown High School students
have charged that the town of Wallkill

1785
01:33:31,314 --> 01:33:34,692
is covering up
by proceeding with the closure,

1786
01:33:34,775 --> 01:33:38,654
and has, at times,
publicly called upon the town

1787
01:33:38,738 --> 01:33:42,116
to cease construction on the landfill cap.

1788
01:33:42,199 --> 01:33:44,535
Now, I'd like to make this very clear.

1789
01:33:44,619 --> 01:33:47,955
This course of action would be illegal.

1790
01:33:48,039 --> 01:33:51,334
Federal and state law mandates

1791
01:33:51,417 --> 01:33:55,588
that we do exactly
what we are doing at this time.

1792
01:33:56,839 --> 01:33:59,508
{\an8}Uh, with that, my comments are completed.

1793
01:33:59,592 --> 01:34:01,510
{\an8}We'll now open the meeting
to public comment.

1794
01:34:01,594 --> 01:34:04,597
Anyone who'd like to speak,
uh, is welcome to do so.

1795
01:34:06,307 --> 01:34:09,977
[Fred] To me, there was something
about the story that was universal,

1796
01:34:10,061 --> 01:34:14,440
that had an appeal beyond its own time.

1797
01:34:14,940 --> 01:34:16,400
Hi, my name is Jonathan Wise.

1798
01:34:16,484 --> 01:34:18,235
-Daniel Andersen.
-Jackie Kordiak.

1799
01:34:18,319 --> 01:34:20,529
Hello. [clears throat]
My name is Melissa Kubik.

1800
01:34:20,613 --> 01:34:21,864
-I'm a sophomore…
-I'm a junior…

1801
01:34:21,947 --> 01:34:23,783
I'm a senior at Middletown High School.

1802
01:34:23,866 --> 01:34:25,910
[Fred] And I think it exemplified things

1803
01:34:25,993 --> 01:34:29,246
that we're taught
to believe are important.

1804
01:34:29,330 --> 01:34:31,832
-[man] Good evening. How you doing?
-All right, how you doing? Um…

1805
01:34:31,916 --> 01:34:33,459
-My name is Jeff Dutemple.
-Welcome back.

1806
01:34:33,542 --> 01:34:35,461
-Huh?
-It's one of the graduates.

1807
01:34:35,544 --> 01:34:37,463
-Welcome back.
-Thank you very much.

1808
01:34:38,673 --> 01:34:42,176
[Fred] One would be
this idea of civic courage.

1809
01:34:42,259 --> 01:34:44,845
My question is in reference
to a quote you made

1810
01:34:44,929 --> 01:34:47,348
when we had you
into the media center for an interview.

1811
01:34:47,431 --> 01:34:54,105
You said that you had no problem hiring
an allegedly Ma-- Mafia-connected company.

1812
01:34:54,188 --> 01:34:57,650
And, um, I was just wondering
how the rest of the board felt about that.

1813
01:34:58,359 --> 01:35:01,696
And that became
one of our-- our mottos, civic courage.

1814
01:35:01,779 --> 01:35:07,326
It means acting as if you live
in a real democracy.

1815
01:35:07,410 --> 01:35:09,328
Was public health
any real concern of yours?

1816
01:35:09,412 --> 01:35:13,666
Because I know that the standards
are a minimum, but you can go beyond that.

1817
01:35:13,749 --> 01:35:15,626
Have you ever lost confidence in the DEC?

1818
01:35:15,710 --> 01:35:17,753
We've noticed there's
always a conflict of interest

1819
01:35:17,837 --> 01:35:19,422
between engineers and the town.

1820
01:35:19,505 --> 01:35:21,757
I know that your family has lived
in the town of Wallkill

1821
01:35:21,841 --> 01:35:23,175
for the past 200 years.

1822
01:35:23,259 --> 01:35:25,594
I was just wondering,
what is the legacy you'll be leaving

1823
01:35:25,678 --> 01:35:27,680
the generations of your family
in the future?

1824
01:35:28,806 --> 01:35:30,015
Next question.

1825
01:35:30,099 --> 01:35:32,560
[Fred] So we knew that a lot
of the things that we were doing

1826
01:35:32,643 --> 01:35:36,188
weren't going to bear any fruit
in terms of getting anybody arrested

1827
01:35:36,272 --> 01:35:38,733
or making any changes in policy.

1828
01:35:38,816 --> 01:35:43,487
But we proceeded
as though this is how it works.

1829
01:35:43,571 --> 01:35:46,991
If you provide evidence,
if you provide proof of something,

1830
01:35:47,616 --> 01:35:49,410
the right thing is going to be done.

1831
01:35:49,493 --> 01:35:50,703
[Jeff whispers inaudibly]

1832
01:35:50,786 --> 01:35:52,955
Mr. Isseks, Mr. Isseks hasn't gone yet.

1833
01:35:53,038 --> 01:35:54,373
I wasn't going to say a word.

1834
01:35:54,457 --> 01:35:57,209
I was just going to sit
in the back and watch. But here I am.

1835
01:35:57,293 --> 01:36:00,588
The fact that we have
national media here tonight,

1836
01:36:00,671 --> 01:36:02,465
I ask myself the question,

1837
01:36:02,548 --> 01:36:05,384
"What can a national audience learn
from this whole thing?"

1838
01:36:06,218 --> 01:36:08,137
So when you point out
that your hands are tied

1839
01:36:08,220 --> 01:36:10,181
and it's the DEC that's doing this,

1840
01:36:10,264 --> 01:36:11,974
I think it's a point well taken.

1841
01:36:12,057 --> 01:36:15,644
And now at a time when Governor Pataki
is looking to cut the DEC,

1842
01:36:15,728 --> 01:36:18,355
and whatever power it has now
is going to be lessened,

1843
01:36:18,939 --> 01:36:22,234
and when Congress is looking
to do the same thing to the EPA,

1844
01:36:22,318 --> 01:36:25,696
I hope that the American people learn
that it's going to be folks like us

1845
01:36:25,780 --> 01:36:27,615
that are gonna have
to take charge of things.

1846
01:36:27,698 --> 01:36:29,533
-Okay.
-And for that, I thank you.

1847
01:36:29,617 --> 01:36:31,702
[townspeople applaud]

1848
01:36:31,786 --> 01:36:33,329
[student] Turn it off now.

1849
01:36:34,205 --> 01:36:36,207
[people chatter]

1850
01:36:39,251 --> 01:36:41,253
[gentle music plays]

1851
01:37:06,570 --> 01:37:08,572
[students chatter excitedly on TV]

1852
01:37:21,085 --> 01:37:24,505
-[Jeff] Rachel! Whoo!
-[Rachel laughs]

1853
01:37:25,381 --> 01:37:27,174
Oh, my gosh.

1854
01:37:27,258 --> 01:37:28,926
-Hi.
-Aw.

1855
01:37:29,009 --> 01:37:30,553
[Rachel] Aww.

1856
01:37:31,679 --> 01:37:33,013
[Fred] So much has changed

1857
01:37:33,097 --> 01:37:36,308
since we all last hung out
in this room together.

1858
01:37:36,392 --> 01:37:38,060
[Rachel] It's crazy, right?

1859
01:37:39,311 --> 01:37:43,148
When you leave your favorite room
in high school, you just walk out.

1860
01:37:43,232 --> 01:37:45,109
[Mike] You think you're never
gonna see it again.

1861
01:37:45,192 --> 01:37:46,777
[Rachel] We'll never see it again.

1862
01:37:50,197 --> 01:37:54,827
I mean, I think this space put everybody
on a level playing field where…

1863
01:37:58,247 --> 01:37:59,498
[chuckles]

1864
01:37:59,582 --> 01:38:00,457
Um…

1865
01:38:02,710 --> 01:38:04,086
-[Mike] Need a hug, buddy?
-Maybe.

1866
01:38:04,169 --> 01:38:06,171
-[TV plays]
-[Fred speaks indistinctly]

1867
01:38:07,464 --> 01:38:08,799
[Jeff] It was a community.

1868
01:38:10,217 --> 01:38:13,220
It's one of the only reasons
I came to school.

1869
01:38:15,264 --> 01:38:16,724
Oh, my gosh.

1870
01:38:18,350 --> 01:38:19,852
This is so…

1871
01:38:19,935 --> 01:38:20,936
[Fred sighs]

1872
01:38:21,020 --> 01:38:22,521
[hopeful music plays]

1873
01:38:22,605 --> 01:38:24,690
-[Fred and Rachel laugh]
-[Mike gasps]

1874
01:38:24,773 --> 01:38:26,692
[Rachel] Oh, my gosh!

1875
01:38:26,775 --> 01:38:28,652
-Oh, my gosh!
-[all laugh]

1876
01:38:28,736 --> 01:38:30,529
-[Mike] This is incredible.
-[Rachel] Right?

1877
01:38:30,613 --> 01:38:32,781
[Fred] I-- I think I remember this.

1878
01:38:32,865 --> 01:38:34,491
[past Rachel] <i>This is a true story.</i>

1879
01:38:34,575 --> 01:38:36,118
[student in sing-song] <i>True story!</i>

1880
01:38:36,201 --> 01:38:39,663
[boy 1] <i>Of determined high school students</i>
<i>who chose to work for a cause.</i>

1881
01:38:39,747 --> 01:38:41,332
[boy 2] <i>By using camcorders and tapes.</i>

1882
01:38:41,415 --> 01:38:44,335
-[girl] <i>To find out what happens.</i>
-[boy 3] <i>When people stop standing around.</i>

1883
01:38:44,418 --> 01:38:46,629
[boy 4] <i>And start making a difference.</i>

1884
01:38:46,712 --> 01:38:48,714
[all] This is the real world.

1885
01:38:48,797 --> 01:38:50,174
[laughing]

1886
01:38:50,257 --> 01:38:54,136
Everyone who's ever been in charge
at the town hall has said the same thing.

1887
01:38:54,219 --> 01:38:57,014
"Don't worry about that dump.
We'll take care of it."

1888
01:38:57,097 --> 01:38:58,682
"It doesn't pose a threat."

1889
01:38:58,766 --> 01:39:02,061
No one wanted to look into the problem.
Politicians didn't want to get involved.

1890
01:39:02,144 --> 01:39:04,063
District attorney wouldn't
even touch the issue.

1891
01:39:04,146 --> 01:39:05,898
Louis Mongelli used to live here.

1892
01:39:05,981 --> 01:39:08,025
He's now in
the Federal Witness Protection Program.

1893
01:39:08,108 --> 01:39:10,653
But he has never been charged
with any environmental crimes,

1894
01:39:10,736 --> 01:39:12,488
even though Round Lake was
the largest user

1895
01:39:12,571 --> 01:39:13,906
of the Orange County Landfill.

1896
01:39:13,989 --> 01:39:16,825
[director] Is <i>Garbage, Gangsters and Greed</i>
a good film?

1897
01:39:16,909 --> 01:39:18,702
{\an8}No! [laughs]

1898
01:39:19,620 --> 01:39:21,747
It's a little bit clunky.

1899
01:39:22,373 --> 01:39:23,916
But that's not really the point.

1900
01:39:25,417 --> 01:39:27,670
[Jeff] I think you can watch
<i>Garbage, Gangsters and Greed</i>

1901
01:39:27,753 --> 01:39:32,299
and be amazed
at how messed up the system is.

1902
01:39:32,383 --> 01:39:35,678
And there's a level of jadedness
that-- that comes with…

1903
01:39:36,303 --> 01:39:38,597
like, knowing you're right.

1904
01:39:38,681 --> 01:39:42,518
The Ford Motor Company operated
a huge automobile plant here

1905
01:39:42,601 --> 01:39:45,270
at the New York-New Jersey state border.

1906
01:39:45,980 --> 01:39:49,024
Ford is gone now, but their mess isn't.

1907
01:39:51,652 --> 01:39:55,114
I just thought at 50
I was going to get cancer and die.

1908
01:39:56,073 --> 01:39:57,783
Then I turned 50,

1909
01:39:57,866 --> 01:39:59,827
and I don't have cancer,

1910
01:39:59,910 --> 01:40:00,995
and I'm healthy.

1911
01:40:01,078 --> 01:40:02,454
I bought a big house,

1912
01:40:02,538 --> 01:40:06,792
and I got a notice
that there's a landfill with toxic waste

1913
01:40:06,875 --> 01:40:08,752
within a few miles of my new house.

1914
01:40:10,379 --> 01:40:13,924
I don't know how much
17-year-old Rachel understood.

1915
01:40:14,842 --> 01:40:18,012
But 50-year-old Rachel now knows

1916
01:40:18,095 --> 01:40:23,350
how important everything
that Fred was teaching us really is.

1917
01:40:24,393 --> 01:40:28,355
Maybe that's why Fred won't give up,
because it's not a story that ever ends.

1918
01:40:28,439 --> 01:40:31,316
Since Ward Stone was thrown
out the dump in 1992,

1919
01:40:31,400 --> 01:40:34,737
no one has done any real tests to see
what pollutants are washing off the site

1920
01:40:34,820 --> 01:40:37,322
and into the wetlands and aquifer below.

1921
01:40:37,406 --> 01:40:40,576
[student] It looks like our children
and our children's children

1922
01:40:40,659 --> 01:40:43,328
will be tending
to this lethal garden forever.

1923
01:40:45,706 --> 01:40:47,708
{\an8}[dreamy music plays]

1924
01:40:51,670 --> 01:40:56,175
{\an8}[David] I don't know. I feel like we had
a good confidence and focus.

1925
01:40:58,010 --> 01:41:00,054
Feel like we were doing pretty good
for kids that age.

1926
01:41:01,722 --> 01:41:05,559
[director] Anything you'd say
to that kid, you know, if you could now?

1927
01:41:05,642 --> 01:41:07,311
Keep going. Keep going!

1928
01:41:07,394 --> 01:41:10,355
Ke-- Keep going, right? Um…

1929
01:41:12,441 --> 01:41:13,817
There's a Rolling Stones song.

1930
01:41:13,901 --> 01:41:16,153
"Isn't it wise sometimes
to never grow up?"

1931
01:41:16,236 --> 01:41:19,490
Like, what if we could get it right?

1932
01:41:20,532 --> 01:41:24,036
What if society could turn
the world back right-side-up?

1933
01:41:27,289 --> 01:41:30,125
[voice breaks] Yeah.
Very proud of all of us.

1934
01:41:31,919 --> 01:41:34,546
I hope-- I hope Fred's as proud as I am.

1935
01:41:36,924 --> 01:41:38,926
[birds squawk]

1936
01:41:39,009 --> 01:41:41,011
[dreamy music continues]

1937
01:41:43,889 --> 01:41:47,142
[Fred] Landfills almost seem
like a secondary problem

1938
01:41:47,226 --> 01:41:49,353
compared to the warming,

1939
01:41:49,436 --> 01:41:52,231
compared to the way
the oceans are changing,

1940
01:41:52,314 --> 01:41:54,149
the poisoning of the cropland.

1941
01:41:54,233 --> 01:41:58,028
All these things seem to be
more dire and more threatening

1942
01:41:58,112 --> 01:42:00,697
than abandoned landfills at this point.

1943
01:42:04,034 --> 01:42:06,870
But I think the landfill
can stand in for that,

1944
01:42:06,954 --> 01:42:10,457
because it represents
a problem that <i>can </i>be addressed,

1945
01:42:10,541 --> 01:42:11,667
that should be addressed,

1946
01:42:11,750 --> 01:42:14,920
but just… [clicks tongue]
kind of gets kicked off to the side.

1947
01:42:16,088 --> 01:42:18,090
[music turns somber]

1948
01:42:20,050 --> 01:42:24,012
When I returned to school
in the fall of 2006,

1949
01:42:24,096 --> 01:42:28,058
I walked into a windowless room
that looked like a shoebox.

1950
01:42:28,142 --> 01:42:30,894
And all of the stuff
that had been in the studio,

1951
01:42:30,978 --> 01:42:35,649
the equipment, the cameras, the tapes,
boxes, cords, cables, everything,

1952
01:42:35,732 --> 01:42:40,279
kind of just… [exclaims] …plopped down
in a pile in the center of the room.

1953
01:42:40,946 --> 01:42:44,158
And it sure looked
an awful lot like punishment.

1954
01:42:48,078 --> 01:42:49,454
[paper ruffles]

1955
01:42:49,538 --> 01:42:53,333
The ancient Greeks had respect for place.

1956
01:42:54,293 --> 01:42:57,838
It's the Greek concept
of <i>topos </i>versus <i>khôra.</i>

1957
01:42:57,921 --> 01:43:00,674
[Jeff] This was the football field,
and then these were the windows…

1958
01:43:00,757 --> 01:43:02,301
-To the library.
-Yeah.

1959
01:43:02,384 --> 01:43:04,720
[Fred] <i>Topos </i>means a physical place.

1960
01:43:04,803 --> 01:43:06,471
One you can find on a map.

1961
01:43:06,555 --> 01:43:08,515
So was the door to the hallway there?

1962
01:43:08,599 --> 01:43:09,975
Oh! There was a door…

1963
01:43:10,058 --> 01:43:12,102
Yeah, there was one over
by that editing room.

1964
01:43:12,186 --> 01:43:13,645
You came in, or the other side.

1965
01:43:13,729 --> 01:43:15,355
It was the whole end of the building.

1966
01:43:16,356 --> 01:43:20,444
[Fred] Whereas <i>khôra</i>
is the specificity of a place,

1967
01:43:20,527 --> 01:43:23,989
and what the place opens up as a theater,

1968
01:43:24,072 --> 01:43:26,575
as a-- a space for creation,

1969
01:43:26,658 --> 01:43:31,079
as, uh, a womb, almost,
for the birth of something.

1970
01:43:31,163 --> 01:43:33,957
[director] Still rolling, guys.
Still listening, looking at the video.

1971
01:43:34,041 --> 01:43:37,127
["Parade" by Kevin Morby plays]

1972
01:43:40,255 --> 01:43:44,176
<i>♪ If you come to find out who you are ♪</i>

1973
01:43:46,220 --> 01:43:50,599
<i>♪ Then may you find out</i>
<i>May you find out who you are </i>♪

1974
01:43:52,851 --> 01:43:56,980
<i>♪ And if you come search</i>
<i>For what is lost ♪</i>

1975
01:43:59,024 --> 01:44:03,737
<i>♪ Then may you find it</i>
<i>May you find it at any cost ♪</i>

1976
01:44:05,739 --> 01:44:10,077
<i>♪ And if you come, oh, so soft ♪</i>

1977
01:44:11,620 --> 01:44:15,999
<i>♪ You may look up one day</i>
<i>And you'll be hard ♪</i>

1978
01:44:21,838 --> 01:44:23,090
<i>♪ If I were… ♪</i>

1979
01:44:23,173 --> 01:44:26,176
David came back to the hotel yesterday,

1980
01:44:26,260 --> 01:44:30,806
and he mentioned that you were considering
the name <i>Garbage, Gangsters and Greed 2.</i>

1981
01:44:30,889 --> 01:44:33,517
And it put a big smile on my face.
I love it.

1982
01:44:33,600 --> 01:44:35,477
I th-- I think that's fantastic.

1983
01:44:35,560 --> 01:44:39,106
I'm waiting for
<i>Garbage, Gangsters and Greed 3, 4, </i>and <i>5.</i>

1984
01:44:39,189 --> 01:44:40,107
[laughs]

1985
01:44:40,983 --> 01:44:45,237
<i>♪ Putting my body on display ♪</i>

1986
01:44:46,363 --> 01:44:48,365
<i>♪ In the parade ♪</i>

1987
01:45:32,576 --> 01:45:37,998
<i>♪ All my friends were there ♪</i>

1988
01:45:38,665 --> 01:45:43,420
<i>♪ Waiting on me ♪</i>

1989
01:45:45,213 --> 01:45:51,511
<i>♪ Oh, Laurie was there ♪</i>

1990
01:45:51,595 --> 01:45:56,350
<i>♪ Waiting on me ♪</i>

1991
01:45:57,684 --> 01:46:00,645
<i>♪ And Anna ♪</i>

1992
01:46:01,646 --> 01:46:03,648
<i>♪ Was there ♪</i>

1993
01:46:04,316 --> 01:46:09,404
<i>♪ Waiting on me ♪</i>

1994
01:46:10,072 --> 01:46:15,952
<i>♪ I've come and I will go ♪</i>

1995
01:46:16,495 --> 01:46:19,206
<i>♪ Just the same ♪</i>

1996
01:46:26,880 --> 01:46:31,051
<i>♪ If I were to die today ♪</i>

1997
01:46:33,220 --> 01:46:37,265
{\an8}<i>♪ Puppet in that gray charade ♪</i>

1998
01:46:39,559 --> 01:46:44,398
{\an8}<i>♪ Last thing that you hear me say ♪</i>

1999
01:46:45,607 --> 01:46:50,487
<i>♪ Is "Bury me in different shades ♪</i>

2000
01:46:51,279 --> 01:46:53,615
<i>♪ Of the parade" ♪</i>

2001
01:46:58,662 --> 01:47:03,125
♪ <i>Passing slowly through the town </i>♪

2002
01:47:05,252 --> 01:47:09,339
♪ <i>My feet, they cannot touch the ground </i>♪

2003
01:47:11,383 --> 01:47:15,762
♪ <i>Go now, you'll go but, oh, just how? </i>♪

2004
01:47:17,722 --> 01:47:21,935
<i>♪ Never leave this</i>
<i>Never leave this town ♪</i>

2005
01:47:23,311 --> 01:47:25,730
<i>♪ Of the parade ♪</i>

2006
01:47:35,615 --> 01:47:38,118
{\an8}["I Am A Scientist"
by Guided by Voices plays]

2007
01:47:52,591 --> 01:47:56,553
<i>♪ I am a scientist</i>
<i>I seek to understand me ♪</i>

2008
01:47:56,636 --> 01:48:00,515
<i>♪ All of my impurities</i>
<i>And evils yet unknown ♪</i>

2009
01:48:00,599 --> 01:48:04,853
<i>♪ I am a journalist</i>
<i>I write to you to show you ♪</i>

2010
01:48:04,936 --> 01:48:10,650
<i>♪ I am an incurable</i>
<i>And nothing else behaves like me ♪</i>

2011
01:48:14,529 --> 01:48:17,407
<i>♪ And I know what's right ♪</i>

2012
01:48:18,658 --> 01:48:22,120
<i>♪ But I'm losing sight of the clues ♪</i>

2013
01:48:22,204 --> 01:48:26,458
<i>♪ For which I search</i>
<i>And choose to abuse </i>♪

2014
01:48:26,541 --> 01:48:28,960
♪ <i>To just unlock my mind ♪</i>

2015
01:48:29,044 --> 01:48:33,215
<i>♪ Yeah, and just unlock my mind ♪</i>

2016
01:48:40,222 --> 01:48:44,059
<i>♪ I am a pharmacist</i>
<i>Prescriptions, I will fill you ♪</i>

2017
01:48:44,142 --> 01:48:48,313
<i>♪ Potions, pills, and medicines</i>
<i>To ease your painful lives ♪</i>

2018
01:48:48,396 --> 01:48:52,192
<i>♪ I am a lost soul</i>
<i>I shoot myself with rock and roll ♪</i>

2019
01:48:52,275 --> 01:48:58,031
<i>♪ The hole I dig is bottomless</i>
<i>But nothing else can set me free ♪</i>

2020
01:49:01,743 --> 01:49:04,871
<i>♪ And I know what's right ♪</i>

2021
01:49:05,997 --> 01:49:09,543
<i>♪ But I'm losing sight of the clues ♪</i>

2022
01:49:09,626 --> 01:49:13,755
<i>♪ For which I search</i>
<i>And choose to abuse ♪</i>

2023
01:49:13,838 --> 01:49:16,258
<i>♪ To just unlock my mind ♪</i>

2024
01:49:16,341 --> 01:49:20,845
<i>♪ Yeah, to just unlock my mind ♪</i>

2025
